{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b2a07c14d23dfd35", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tMuseCritic: Learning Multi-Aspect Song Rewards through Natural-Language Aesthetic Critiques \n2\tTitle: \n3\tContent selection saved. Describe the issue below: \n4\tDescription: \n5\t\n6\tarXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × \n7\t\n8\tLicense: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license \n9\tarXiv:2608.11755v1 [cs.SD] 12 Aug 2026 \n10\t\n11\t† † * Equal contribution. † Corresponding authors \n12\tMuseCritic: Learning Multi-Aspect Song Rewards through Natural-Language Aesthetic Critiques \n13\t\n14\tJiabao Zhuang *,† , Changhao Jiang * , Hanchen Wang * , \n15\tJiahao Chen * , Zhixiong Yang * , Zhenghao Xiang * , \n16\tYifei Cao,\n17\tJiajun Sun,\n18\tHui Li,\n19\tMing Zhang, \n20\tTao Ji,\n21\tTao Gui † ,\n22\tQi Zhang,\n23\tXuanjing Huang \n24\tFudan NLP Group, Fudan University \n25\t25213050071@m.fudan.edu.cn, tgui@fudan.edu.cn \n26\t\n27\tAbstract \n28\tLong-form song generation models continue to improve in duration, structural integrity, and acoustic complexity, making reliable aesthetic rewards increasingly important for aligning these models with human preferences. However, reward models for complete songs remain limited, and existing evaluators typically predict scores in a single forward pass without providing readable explanations. To this end, we introduce MuseCritic, a semi-scalar reward model that generates a natural-language critique covering five aesthetic dimensions and uses it as an intermediate representation to predict continuous reward scores. MuseCritic follows a two-stage training pipeline: a teacher model first provides high-quality critiques for supervised fine-tuning, after which the fine-tuned model generates its own critiques for reward learning, mitigating distribution shift between training and inference. On an in-domain test set of 200 SongEval songs, MuseCritic reduces macro-averaged mean squared error from 0.2875 to 0.2316 and improves macro-averaged LCC, SRCC, and Kendall’s τ \\tau to 0.9068, 0.8838, and 0.7178, respectively. On the out-of-domain Music Arena benchmark with 733 preference pairs, it achieves the highest accuracy of 71.35%. Moreover, using MuseCritic with GRPO improves Muse-0.6B on all nine aesthetic metrics from SongEval and Audiobox Aesthetics. These results demonstrate that critique-conditioned reward modeling reduces scoring error and provides an effective optimization signal for song generation. The project repository is available at https://github.com/WuqnEl/MuseCritic . \n29\t\n30\t1 Introduction \n31\t\n32\tText-to-music generation has progressed from synthesizing short clips to composing complete songs. Early systems generate musical excerpts from natural-language descriptions ( 1 ; 5 ) , while recent systems—including DiffRhythm, YuE, LeVo, ACE-Step, and Muse—support minutes-long vocal music, lyric alignment, and fine-grained style control ( 17 ; 25 ; 14 ; 7 ; 12 ) . As generation length and acoustic realism improve, reliable aesthetic evaluation becomes increasingly important for model selection and for providing optimization signals aligned with human preferences. \n33\t\n34\tExisting evaluators, however, remain limited for complete songs. Objective metrics such as phoneme error rate, MuLan, and MuQ-MuLan capture lyric intelligibility or audio–text correspondence rather than perceptual and artistic quality ( 11 ; 28 ) . PAM, MusicEval, and Audiobox Aesthetics broaden evaluation to audio quality and musical aesthetics ( 6 ; 15 ; 20 ) , while SongEval provides five expert aesthetic criteria for full-length synthetic songs ( 23 ) ; nevertheless, these evaluators map audio directly to scores and do not explain the audible evidence behind their judgments. In contrast, LLM-as-a-Judge and critique-conditioned reward models show that language models can interpret rubrics and generate corresponding rationales or supporting reasoning before scoring textual responses, thereby improving textual preference modeling ( 27 ; 2 ; 24 ; 26 ; 16 ) . Whether this linguistic intermediate representation can improve multidimensional aesthetic reward modeling for complete songs remains underexplored. Existing audio reward models predict scores directly, while general-purpose multimodal models lack task-specific training and show weaker agreement with expert judgments. \n35\t\n36\tFigure 1: Overview of the two-stage training and inference pipeline of MuseCritic. In Stage I, Gemini-3-Pro generates aesthetic critiques from songs, evaluation rubrics, and expert mean ratings to construct 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} , which is used to fine-tune a pretrained large language model into an SFT critique generator. In Stage II, the SFT model replaces the teacher critiques with one self-generated five-aspect critique per song to construct 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} ; a scalar reward head is then initialized, and MuseCritic is trained using expert mean ratings as regression targets. At inference time, MuseCritic first generates a five-aspect critique and then predicts five continuous aesthetic scores conditioned on the song, rubric, and self-generated critique. \n37\t\n38\tIn this work, we investigate explicit linguistic reasoning in song reward modeling, enabling the model to form a representation of aesthetic evidence aligned with the evaluation rubric before predicting multidimensional scores. To this end, we propose MuseCritic, a semi-scalar reward model built around natural-language aesthetic critiques. Given a song and a five-dimensional evaluation rubric, MuseCritic generates a single critique containing five labeled analyses: Overall Coherence, Memorability, Naturalness of Vocal Breathing and Phrasing, Clarity of Song Structure, and Overall Musicality. Conditioned on the song, rubric, and self-generated critique, a reward head then predicts five continuous aesthetic scores ranging from 1 to 5. The critique is not a post-hoc text appended to predicted scores; it is an explicit intermediate variable connecting song representations to rewards. This design introduces a variable-length textual representation before prediction and organizes auditory evidence distributed across a long song into dimension-aligned descriptions. \n39\t\n40\tFigure 1 summarizes the training and inference pipeline. The model first learns five-dimensional aesthetic analysis from critiques generated by an external teacher. It then generates self-generated critiques for the training songs and performs reward learning on the resulting dataset. \n41\t\n42\tAcross both in-domain and out-of-domain evaluations, critique-conditioned reward modeling consistently improves agreement with human judgments. On the held-out SongEval test set, MuseCritic reduces macro-averaged MSE from 0.2875 to 0.2316 and raises macro-averaged LCC, SRCC, and Kendall’s τ \\tau to 0.9068, 0.8838, and 0.7178. On Music Arena, MuseCritic attains the highest observed pairwise accuracy of 71.35% across 733 preference pairs. When used as the reward model for GRPO, it further improves Muse-0.6B on all nine aesthetic metrics reported by SongEval and Audiobox Aesthetics. \n43\t\n44\tOur contributions are threefold: \n45\t\n46\t• \n47\t\n48\tWe introduce MuseCritic, a semi-scalar reward model for complete songs. It generates a natural-language critique containing five dimension-specific analyses and uses this critique to predict five continuous scores, combining verbal evaluation and reward estimation within a single model (Figure 1 ). \n49\t\n50\t• \n51\t\n52\tWe systematically evaluate MuseCritic on in-domain SongEval ratings and out-of-domain Music Arena preferences. Self-generated critiques improve agreement with expert scores and mitigate the critique distribution shift between reward training and inference (Tables 1 , 2 , and 4 ). \n53\t\n54\t• \n55\t\n56\tWe use MuseCritic as the reward model for GRPO training of Muse-0.6B. The resulting policy improves on all nine observed metrics from SongEval and Audiobox Aesthetics, showing that the learned reward provides a useful optimization signal (Table 3 ). \n57\t\n58\t2 Related Work \n59\t\n60\t2.1 Text-to-Song Generation \n61\t\n62\tAdvances in neural audio codecs and large-scale audio generation have moved language-conditioned music synthesis from early systems such as MusicLM and MusicGen toward recent systems capable of generating complete songs ( 1 ; 5 ) .\n63\tUnlike short musical excerpts, complete songs require cross-section structure, coordination between vocals and accompaniment, and long-range lyric alignment. \n64\t\n65\tRecent systems can generate complete songs several minutes in duration.\n66\tDiffRhythm performs end-to-end long-form song synthesis through latent diffusion ( 17 ) ; YuE combines dual-track tokenization with progressive structural conditioning to generate songs up to five minutes long ( 25 ) ; and ACE-Step integrates diffusion with musical semantic representations ( 7 ) .\n67\tLeVo and Muse further support vocal–accompaniment coordination and fine-grained, multi-turn style control ( 14 ; 12 ) .\n68\tMusicRL learns music rewards from human feedback ( 4 ) , while LeVo uses multi-preference DPO to improve song quality and instruction following ( 14 ) .\n69\tAs complete-song generation matures, reliable aesthetic evaluation and reward signals suitable for reinforcement learning become central challenges.\n70\tOur work focuses on the reward itself, enabling one model to both describe five-dimensional aesthetic quality and assign continuous rewards. \n71\t\n72\t2.2 Reward Modeling and Music Aesthetic Evaluation \n73\t\n74\tClassical RLHF reward models attach a shallow scalar head to a pretrained language-model backbone to fit human preferences ( 18 ) , whereas LLM-as-a-Judge uses capable language models to produce evaluations and supporting rationales ( 27 ) .\n75\tSemi-scalar and generative reward models seek to combine linguistic explanation with numerical prediction.\n76\tCLoud generates natural-language critiques before predicting rewards ( 2 ) ; Critic-RM jointly trains self-generated critiques and reward prediction ( 24 ) ; and Generative Verifiers and DeepSeek-GRM improve reward judgments through reasoning traces and test-time sampling ( 26 ; 16 ) .\n77\tThese approaches primarily evaluate text responses.\n78\tMuseCritic extends this modeling paradigm to long-form song audio and multidimensional continuous aesthetic assessment. \n79\t\n80\tExisting music evaluators typically predict proxy metrics or scalar scores.\n81\tMuLan and MuQ-MuLan measure text–music correspondence ( 11 ; 28 ) ; PAM estimates reference-free audio quality ( 6 ) ; MusicEval trains a CLAP-based evaluator on expert ratings of generated music clips ( 15 ) ; and Audiobox Aesthetics predicts content enjoyment, content usefulness, production complexity, and production quality ( 20 ) .\n82\tSongEval defines five dimensions for complete Chinese and English songs: coherence, memorability, vocal naturalness, structural clarity, and musicality ( 23 ) , but still targets score prediction alone.\n83\tMuseCritic adopts the SongEval rubric while retaining the critique-generation capability of an audio language model, allowing explicit aesthetic evidence to participate in reward prediction. \n84\t\n85\t3 Method \n86\t\n87\t3.1 Preliminaries \n88\t\n89\tProblem formulation. \n90\t\n91\tLet x ∈ 𝒳 x\\in\\mathcal{X} denote a complete song with vocals, p p an aesthetic-evaluation prompt that defines the assessment dimensions, and K = 5 K=5 the number of dimensions.\n92\tThe expert annotation is 𝐲 ∗ = [ y 1 ∗ , … , y K ∗ ] ∈ [ 1 , 5 ] K \\mathbf{y}^{*}=[y^{*}_{1},\\ldots,y^{*}_{K}]\\in[1,5]^{K} .\n93\tThe five dimensions are Overall Coherence, Memorability, Naturalness of Vocal Breathing and Phrasing, Clarity of Song Structure, and Overall Musicality ( 23 ) .\n94\tWe use an instruction-tuned audio language model f θ f_{\\theta} as the shared backbone of our semi-scalar reward model.\n95\tIts critique generator g c g_{c} and aesthetic scorer g s g_{s} are \n96\t\n97\tg c = h LM ∘ f θ , g s = h RM ∘ f θ , g_{c}=h_{\\mathrm{LM}}\\circ f_{\\theta},\\qquad g_{s}=h_{\\mathrm{RM}}\\circ f_{\\theta}, \n98\t\n99\t(1) \n100\t\n101\twhere h LM h_{\\mathrm{LM}} is a language-modeling head that generates a natural-language aesthetic critique, and h RM h_{\\mathrm{RM}} is a reward-modeling head that produces K K continuous aesthetic scores. The two components always share the same backbone parameters θ \\theta . \n102\t\n103\tReward modeling. \n104\t\n105\tUnlike reward models that predict a scalar directly from audio, the aesthetic scorer of MuseCritic jointly conditions on the song x x , evaluation rubric p p , and aesthetic critique c c : \n106\t\n107\t𝐲 ^ = g s ​ ( x , p , c ) . \\hat{\\mathbf{y}}=g_{s}(x,p,c). \n108\t\n109\t(2) \n110\t\n111\tFor any set 𝒮 \\mathcal{S} of expert-annotated samples, we learn the five-dimensional scores by minimizing mean squared error: \n112\t\n113\tℒ MSE ​ ( 𝒮 , g s ) \\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{MSE}}(\\mathcal{S};g_{s}) \n114\t= 1 | 𝒮 | ​ K ∑ ( x , p , c , 𝐲 ∗ ) ∈ 𝒮 \\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{S}|K}\\sum_{(x,p,c,\\mathbf{y}^{*})\\in\\mathcal{S}} \n115\t\n116\t(3) \n117\t\n118\t‖ g s ​ ( x , p , c ) − 𝐲 ∗ ‖ 2 2 . \\displaystyle\\left\\|g_{s}(x,p,c)-\\mathbf{y}^{*}\\right\\|_{2}^{2}. \n119\t\n120\tThis objective simultaneously constrains all five dimensions to align the predictions with expert ratings. \n121\t\n122\t3.2 The MuseCritic Reward Model \n123\t\n124\tOverview. \n125\t\n126\tTo introduce an explicit textual intermediate representation into reward modeling, we treat natural-language aesthetic critiques as intermediate variables between a song and its final scores.\n127\tGiven a song x x and rubric p p , MuseCritic first generates \n128\t\n129\tc ^ = g c ​ ( x , p ) , \\hat{c}=g_{c}(x,p), \n130\t\n131\t(4) \n132\t\n133\twhere c ^ \\hat{c} is a single critique with five labeled sections describing perceptible musical phenomena under the corresponding dimensions.\n134\tThe shared backbone then receives the song, rubric, and self-generated critique to produce \n135\t\n136\t𝐲 ^ = g s ​ ( x , p , c ^ ) . \\hat{\\mathbf{y}}=g_{s}(x,p,\\hat{c}). \n137\t\n138\t(5) \n139\t\n140\tRelative to direct score prediction, Equation 5 introduces a variable-length textual representation before scoring and organizes descriptions of auditory evidence distributed across a long song according to the evaluation rubric. \n141\t\n142\tTraining MuseCritic. \n143\t\n144\tIdeally, MuseCritic would be trained on a dataset 𝒟 ∗ = { ( x i , p , c i ∗ , 𝐲 i ∗ ) } i = 1 N \\mathcal{D}^{*}=\\{(x_{i},p,c_{i}^{*},\\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*})\\}_{i=1}^{N} , where c i ∗ c_{i}^{*} is a human-authored aesthetic critique consistent with the corresponding expert ratings.\n145\tSuch critiques would be authored by human music experts, but existing song datasets rarely pair expert scores with expert critiques.\n146\tFollowing prior work that constructs supervision from model feedback ( 3 ; 2 ) , we use the audio-capable Gemini-3-Pro ( 9 ) to generate score-conditioned synthetic critiques.\n147\tSpecifically, we begin with a score-only dataset 𝒟 score = { ( x i , 𝐲 i ∗ ) } i = 1 N \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{score}}=\\{(x_{i},\\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*})\\}_{i=1}^{N} .\n148\tTo generate a critique, Gemini observes the song x i x_{i} , rubric p p , and expert mean ratings 𝐲 i ∗ \\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*} , then writes dimension-specific commentary whose evaluative polarity is consistent with each rating on a five-point Likert scale.\n149\tThe prompt requests analysis of concrete musical phenomena while prohibiting numerical scores in the critique itself.\n150\tThis procedure yields the offline critique dataset \n151\t\n152\t𝒟 off = { ( x i , p , c i off , 𝐲 i ∗ ) } i = 1 N . \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}}=\\{(x_{i},p,c_{i}^{\\mathrm{off}},\\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*})\\}_{i=1}^{N}. \n153\t\n154\t(6) \n155\t\n156\tAppendix D provides the complete external-teacher prompt.\n157\tTo further ensure the reliability of the aesthetic critiques, we manually verify the generated critiques, as detailed in Appendix A . \n158\t\n159\tTraining comprises two stages.\n160\tIn Stage I, we supervise the audio language model on 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} with the critique-generation objective \n161\t\n162\tℒ SFT = \\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{SFT}}={} \n163\t− 1 ∑ i = 1 N | c i off | \\displaystyle-\\frac{1}{\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\lvert c_{i}^{\\mathrm{off}}\\rvert} \n164\t\n165\t(7) \n166\t\n167\t∑ i = 1 N ∑ t = 1 | c i off | log ⁡ p θ ​ ( c i , t off ∣ x i , p , c i , < t off ) . \\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\sum_{t=1}^{\\lvert c_{i}^{\\mathrm{off}}\\rvert}\\log p_{\\theta}\\!\\left(c_{i,t}^{\\mathrm{off}}\\mid x_{i},p,c_{i,<t}^{\\mathrm{off}}\\right). \n168\t\n169\tThis is the standard next-token cross-entropy averaged over non-masked critique target tokens; the audio and user-prompt tokens do not contribute to the loss.\n170\tThis stage produces a critique generator g c ′ g^{\\prime}_{c} with the ability to analyze a song along the five aesthetic dimensions. \n171\t\n172\tIn Stage II, we first use g c ′ g^{\\prime}_{c} to generate a new critique for every training example: \n173\t\n174\tc i on = g c ′ ​ ( x i , p ) . c_{i}^{\\mathrm{on}}=g^{\\prime}_{c}(x_{i},p). \n175\t\n176\t(8) \n177\t\n178\tWe replace the external-teacher critiques with these self-generated critiques and call the resulting collection the self-generated critique dataset 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} : \n179\t\n180\t𝒟 on = { ( x i , p , c i on , 𝐲 i ∗ ) } i = 1 N . \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}}=\\{(x_{i},p,c_{i}^{\\mathrm{on}},\\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*})\\}_{i=1}^{N}. \n181\t\n182\t(9) \n183\t\n184\tWe then initialize the shared MuseCritic backbone from the SFT model and jointly update the backbone LoRA parameters and all reward-head parameters on 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} .\n185\tThe reward model thus learns from critiques generated by the target model family rather than by the external teacher, mitigating the critique distribution shift between reward training and inference.\n186\tThe objective is the MSE from Equation 3 applied to 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} : \n187\t\n188\tℒ MC = ℒ MSE ​ ( 𝒟 on , g s ) . \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{MC}}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{MSE}}(\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}};g_{s}). \n189\t\n190\t(10) \n191\t\n192\tAt inference time, the final LoRA-updated MuseCritic backbone generates an aesthetic critique according to Equation 4 and then predicts five rewards according to Equation 5 . \n193\t\n194\t4 Experiments \n195\t\n196\t4.1 Experimental Setup \n197\t\n198\tData. \n199\t\n200\tWe use SongEval ( 23 ) for training and in-domain evaluation.\n201\tIt contains 2,399 complete Chinese and English songs totaling more than 140 hours of audio, with five-dimensional aesthetic scores from 16 annotators with music expertise.\n202\tThe data span nine major genres, including pop, rock, and electronic music.\n203\tBecause the original work does not release a fixed train–test split, we shuffle all samples with seed 42, use 2,199 songs for training, and reserve the remaining 200 for testing.\n204\tEvery experiment uses this split.\n205\tFor the training set, Gemini-3-Pro ( 9 ) observes each song and its expert mean ratings to generate a single critique containing five dimension-specific analyses, producing 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} .\n206\tThe SFT model subsequently regenerates one five-part critique per song with greedy decoding ( T = 0 T=0 , no sampling), producing the self-generated critique dataset 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} .\n207\tAppendices D and A provide the teacher and model prompts and the splitting algorithm. \n208\t\n209\tFor out-of-domain evaluation, we use the 733 song-preference pairs from Music Arena ( 13 ) .\n210\tThis dataset contains outputs from systems not represented in SongEval and provides pairwise human preferences rather than five-dimensional absolute scores.\n211\tIt therefore measures preference generalization under distribution shift. \n212\t\n213\tBaselines. \n214\t\n215\tThe in-domain task requires five-dimensional song aesthetic scores.\n216\tWe compare against two classes of baselines: (1) Gemini-3.1-Pro ( 8 ) , an audio-capable LLM-as-a-Judge that generates critiques and five-dimensional ratings under the same rubric; and (2) SongEval (UTMOS) , a discriminative reward model that directly regresses five scores from audio.\n217\tSongEval does not release its original training code or data split, so we reimplement its published method and UTMOS architecture and train it for ten epochs on our 2,199/200 split, controlling for differences in training data. \n218\t\n219\tMusic Arena requires pairwise preference prediction.\n220\tIn addition to MuseCritic, we evaluate the officially released SongEval weights ( 23 ) , the general-purpose audio aesthetic model Audiobox Aesthetics ( 20 ) , and the open-source audio language model Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B-Instruct ( 21 ) .\n221\tQwen3-Omni uses the same rubric and generative scoring format as the in-domain Gemini baseline. \n222\t\n223\tMetrics. \n224\t\n225\tFor in-domain evaluation, we report dimension-wise mean squared error (MSE), Pearson’s linear correlation coefficient (LCC), Spearman’s rank correlation coefficient (SRCC), and Kendall’s τ \\tau (KTAU).\n226\tMSE measures agreement with the experts’ absolute rating scale, while the three correlation coefficients measure relative agreement across songs in terms of linear association and ranking.\n227\tEach metric is computed independently over the 200 test songs for each dimension. \n228\t\n229\tFor out-of-domain evaluation, we report pairwise accuracy.\n230\tA prediction is correct only when the model assigns a strictly higher overall reward to the human-preferred song; ties count as errors.\n231\tFor SongEval, Audiobox Aesthetics, and MuseCritic, we average the respective output dimensions with equal weight.\n232\tFor Qwen3-Omni, we parse its five generated ratings under the shared rubric and take their mean.\n233\tAll main evaluations use deterministic decoding with T = 0 T=0 . \n234\t\n235\tFor downstream evaluation after GRPO, music quality is assessed using SongEval ( 23 ) in terms of overall coherence (CO), memorability (ME), naturalness of vocal breathing and phrasing (NA), clarity of song structure (CL), and overall musicality (MU).\n236\tAudio quality is evaluated with Audiobox Aesthetics ( 20 ) , which considers content enjoyment (CE), content usefulness (CU), production complexity (PC), and production quality (PQ). \n237\t\n238\tTraining. \n239\t\n240\tAll MuseCritic variants use MOSS-Audio-8B-Instruct ( 22 ) as the base model.\n241\tDuring critique cold-start, we fine-tune all backbone parameters with a learning rate of 5 × 10 − 5 5\\times 10^{-5} and use the checkpoint saved after one epoch.\n242\tWe initialize MuseCritic from this checkpoint and train it for ten epochs on the self-generated critique data.\n243\tThe backbone uses LoRA (rank 8, scaling factor 32) ( 10 ) , while the reward head is fully updated with a learning rate of 2 × 10 − 4 2\\times 10^{-4} .\n244\tBoth optimization stages use four NVIDIA H200 GPUs, a per-device batch size of 1, gradient accumulation over 8 steps (global batch size 32), a 5% warmup, cosine learning-rate scheduling, and a fixed random seed of 42.\n245\tReward learning uses a weight decay of 0.1.\n246\tThe offline-critique and no-critique ablations use exactly the same reward-learning configuration.\n247\tAppendix C lists all hyperparameters. \n248\t\n249\t4.2 Evaluation Results \n250\t\n251\tDimension \n252\tModel \n253\tMSE ↓ \\downarrow \n254\tLCC ↑ \\uparrow \n255\tSRCC ↑ \\uparrow \n256\tKTAU ↑ \\uparrow \n257\t\n258\tCoherence \n259\tMuseCritic \n260\t0.2058 \n261\t0.9147 \n262\t0.8894 \n263\t0.7262 \n264\t\n265\tGemini-3.1-Pro \n266\t0.9881 \n267\t0.5906 \n268\t0.5898 \n269\t0.4359 \n270\t\n271\tSongEval (UTMOS) \n272\t0.2605 \n273\t0.8876 \n274\t0.8605 \n275\t0.6835 \n276\t\n277\tMemorability \n278\tMuseCritic \n279\t0.2511 \n280\t0.9098 \n281\t0.8839 \n282\t0.7232 \n283\t\n284\tGemini-3.1-Pro \n285\t1.1183 \n286\t0.5806 \n287\t0.5623 \n288\t0.4113 \n289\t\n290\tSongEval (UTMOS) \n291\t0.3055 \n292\t0.8857 \n293\t0.8595 \n294\t0.6837 \n295\t\n296\tVocal naturalness \n297\tMuseCritic \n298\t0.2537 \n299\t0.8906 \n300\t0.8678 \n301\t0.6945 \n302\t\n303\tGemini-3.1-Pro \n304\t1.9736 \n305\t0.4921 \n306\t0.5237 \n307\t0.3877 \n308\t\n309\tSongEval (UTMOS) \n310\t0.3071 \n311\t0.8593 \n312\t0.8293 \n313\t0.6491 \n314\t\n315\tStructural clarity \n316\tMuseCritic \n317\t0.2275 \n318\t0.9068 \n319\t0.8806 \n320\t0.7109 \n321\t\n322\tGemini-3.1-Pro \n323\t1.4740 \n324\t0.5532 \n325\t0.5752 \n326\t0.4162 \n327\t\n328\tSongEval (UTMOS) \n329\t0.2730 \n330\t0.8831 \n331\t0.8565 \n332\t0.6790 \n333\t\n334\tMusicality \n335\tMuseCritic \n336\t0.2202 \n337\t0.9120 \n338\t0.8972 \n339\t0.7344 \n340\t\n341\tGemini-3.1-Pro \n342\t0.9763 \n343\t0.6152 \n344\t0.6099 \n345\t0.4459 \n346\t\n347\tSongEval (UTMOS) \n348\t0.2913 \n349\t0.8807 \n350\t0.8599 \n351\t0.6876 \n352\tTable 1: In-domain five-dimensional aesthetic scoring on the SongEval test set. All models are evaluated on the fixed test set of 200 songs. SongEval (UTMOS) denotes the regressor retrained on the same 2,199/200 split. MSE measures absolute error against expert ratings; LCC, SRCC, and KTAU denote Pearson, Spearman, and Kendall τ \\tau correlations. Arrows indicate the preferred direction, and bold marks the best value for each metric and dimension. \n353\t\n354\tModel \n355\tAccuracy (%) ↑ \\uparrow \n356\t\n357\tQwen3-Omni-30B-A3B \n358\t53.75 \n359\t\n360\tAudiobox Aesthetics \n361\t68.49 \n362\t\n363\tSongEval \n364\t70.80 \n365\t\n366\tMuseCritic \n367\t71.35 \n368\tTable 2: Out-of-domain pairwise preference results on Music Arena. The test set contains 733 song pairs with human preference labels. A prediction is correct when the model assigns a higher mean aesthetic score to the human-preferred song; ties count as errors. SongEval uses its officially released weights. MuseCritic achieves the highest observed accuracy. \n369\t\n370\t4.2.1 In-Domain Song Aesthetic Scoring \n371\t\n372\tTable 1 compares model predictions with the expert mean ratings of the 200-song held-out SongEval test set.\n373\tMuseCritic achieves the lowest MSE and highest correlation on all five dimensions.\n374\tRelative to the SongEval (UTMOS) regression baseline trained on the same data, MuseCritic reduces macro-averaged MSE from 0.2875 to 0.2316.\n375\tMacro-averaged LCC, SRCC, and KTAU increase from 0.8793, 0.8531, and 0.6766 to 0.9068, 0.8838, and 0.7178.\n376\tSection 4.4 isolates the effect of critique modeling through controlled ablations. \n377\t\n378\tAlthough Gemini-3.1-Pro has not been specialized for song aesthetics, it generates coherent critiques.\n379\tIts macro-average MSE of 1.3061, however, substantially exceeds the 0.2316 of MuseCritic, and its dimension-wise correlations fall below those of models supervised by expert scores.\n380\tThis result indicates that an unadapted LLM-as-a-Judge cannot reliably reproduce five-dimensional expert judgments of song aesthetics.\n381\tBy supervising the reward head with expert ratings, MuseCritic maps information in the critiques to continuous scores that better align with expert assessments. \n382\t\n383\t4.2.2 Out-of-Domain Preference Generalization \n384\t\n385\tTable 2 reports pairwise preference accuracy on Music Arena.\n386\tMuseCritic achieves the highest observed accuracy of 71.35%, which is 0.55, 2.86, and 17.60 percentage points above SongEval, Audiobox Aesthetics, and Qwen3-Omni, respectively.\n387\tTogether with the in-domain results, this observation suggests that the learned rewards retain ranking information under an out-of-domain preference evaluation. \n388\t\n389\t4.3 Downstream Reinforcement Learning \n390\t\n391\tTo test whether MuseCritic provides a useful optimization signal, we use it to train Muse-0.6B ( 12 ) with GRPO ( 19 ) .\n392\tUsing seed 42, we sample 500 multi-turn song-generation examples from the public Muse training set while preserving the original language ratio: 195 Chinese and 305 English examples.\n393\tWe define the reinforcement-learning reward function as the mean of the five aesthetic scores assigned by MuseCritic to each generated song x ~ \\tilde{x} : \n394\t\n395\tr ⁡ ( x ~ ) = 1 K ​ ∑ k = 1 K y ^ k ​ ( x ~ ) . r(\\tilde{x})=\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\hat{y}_{k}(\\tilde{x}). \n396\t\n397\t(11) \n398\t\n399\tWe train for one epoch on six H200 GPUs, sample eight outputs per prompt, and use a learning rate of 10 − 6 10^{-6} .\n400\tAppendix F gives the complete rollout configuration. \n401\t\n402\tEvaluation uses the 100 multi-turn test prompts from Muse ( 12 ) .\n403\tBoth Muse and Muse-GRPO use greedy decoding with T = 0 T=0 .\n404\tAppendix F describes the complete evaluation protocol and robustness checks across evaluation windows.\n405\tAs shown in Table 3 , Muse-GRPO obtains higher observed values than the original model on all nine Audiobox Aesthetics and SongEval metrics.\n406\tThe largest Audiobox gains occur in production complexity (PC, + 0.15 +0.15 ) and content enjoyment (CE, + 0.12 +0.12 ), while all five SongEval dimensions improve by 0.05 0.05 – 0.06 0.06 .\n407\tThe consistent numerical gains across two song-aesthetic evaluators suggest that the MuseCritic reward captures several aesthetic dimensions. \n408\t\n409\tAudiobox Aesthetics ↑ \\uparrow \n410\tSongEval ↑ \\uparrow \n411\t\n412\tModel \n413\tCE \n414\tCU \n415\tPC \n416\tPQ \n417\tCO \n418\tMU \n419\tME \n420\tCL \n421\tNA \n422\t\n423\tMuse \n424\t7.33 \n425\t7.64 \n426\t6.37 \n427\t8.11 \n428\t4.01 \n429\t3.84 \n430\t3.94 \n431\t3.89 \n432\t3.83 \n433\t\n434\tMuse-GRPO \n435\t7.45 \n436\t7.66 \n437\t6.52 \n438\t8.12 \n439\t4.07 \n440\t3.89 \n441\t4.00 \n442\t3.94 \n443\t3.89 \n444\tTable 3: Effect of MuseCritic-guided GRPO on the aesthetic quality of Muse-0.6B. Results use the 100 multi-turn Muse test prompts and T = 0 T=0 decoding. CE/CU/PC/PQ denote Audiobox content enjoyment, content usefulness, production complexity, and production quality. CO/MU/ME/CL/NA denote SongEval coherence, musicality, memorability, structural clarity, and vocal naturalness. Arrows indicate the preferred direction, and bold marks the better observed result in each column. Muse-GRPO yields numerical gains on all nine metrics. Figure 2: Effect of natural-language aesthetic critiques on five-dimensional score distributions. Curves show expert ratings, full MuseCritic predictions, and predictions from the no-critique variant on the fixed SongEval test set. Coherence, Musicality, Memorability, Clarity, and Naturalness correspond to the five aesthetic dimensions. Without critiques, predictions cluster in a narrow high-score region; the full model more closely matches the range of expert ratings. \n445\t\n446\t4.4 Ablation Studies \n447\t\n448\tTable 4 studies three design choices across all five dimensions: natural-language aesthetic critiques, self-generated critiques, and SFT initialization before reward learning.\n449\tAll variants use the same training songs, reward head, and optimization configuration. \n450\t\n451\tDimension \n452\tModel variant \n453\tMSE ↓ \\downarrow \n454\tLCC ↑ \\uparrow \n455\tSRCC ↑ \\uparrow \n456\tKTAU ↑ \\uparrow \n457\t\n458\tCoherence \n459\tMuseCritic \n460\t0.2058 \n461\t0.9147 \n462\t0.8894 \n463\t0.7262 \n464\t\n465\tw/o critique \n466\t0.3881 \n467\t0.9173 \n468\t0.8954 \n469\t0.7326 \n470\t\n471\tOffline critique \n472\t0.4821 \n473\t0.8342 \n474\t0.7566 \n475\t0.5675 \n476\t\n477\tw/o SFT \n478\t0.8556 \n479\t0.6300 \n480\t0.6066 \n481\t0.4689 \n482\t\n483\tMemorability \n484\tMuseCritic \n485\t0.2511 \n486\t0.9098 \n487\t0.8839 \n488\t0.7232 \n489\t\n490\tw/o critique \n491\t0.4936 \n492\t0.9048 \n493\t0.8842 \n494\t0.7219 \n495\t\n496\tOffline critique \n497\t0.6635 \n498\t0.8141 \n499\t0.7590 \n500\t0.5672 \n501\t\n502\tw/o SFT \n503\t1.0037 \n504\t0.6175 \n505\t0.6378 \n506\t0.4823 \n507\t\n508\tVocal naturalness \n509\tMuseCritic \n510\t0.2537 \n511\t0.8906 \n512\t0.8678 \n513\t0.6945 \n514\t\n515\tw/o critique \n516\t0.6186 \n517\t0.8899 \n518\t0.8684 \n519\t0.7034 \n520\t\n521\tOffline critique \n522\t0.5739 \n523\t0.7936 \n524\t0.7527 \n525\t0.5674 \n526\t\n527\tw/o SFT \n528\t0.6469 \n529\t0.6779 \n530\t0.6768 \n531\t0.4978 \n532\t\n533\tStructural clarity \n534\tMuseCritic \n535\t0.2275 \n536\t0.9068 \n537\t0.8806 \n538\t0.7109 \n539\t\n540\tw/o critique \n541\t0.4635 \n542\t0.9041 \n543\t0.8785 \n544\t0.7088 \n545\t\n546\tOffline critique \n547\t0.5033 \n548\t0.8145 \n549\t0.7479 \n550\t0.5526 \n551\t\n552\tw/o SFT \n553\t0.6681 \n554\t0.6869 \n555\t0.6764 \n556\t0.5061 \n557\t\n558\tMusicality \n559\tMuseCritic \n560\t0.2202 \n561\t0.9120 \n562\t0.8972 \n563\t0.7344 \n564\t\n565\tw/o critique \n566\t0.5386 \n567\t0.9080 \n568\t0.8875 \n569\t0.7293 \n570\t\n571\tOffline critique \n572\t0.5175 \n573\t0.8205 \n574\t0.7765 \n575\t0.5877 \n576\t\n577\tw/o SFT \n578\t0.5960 \n579\t0.7354 \n580\t0.7325 \n581\t0.5488 \n582\tTable 4: In-domain ablations of the key MuseCritic training choices. All variants use the same 2,199/200 SongEval split. “w/o critique” predicts scores directly, “Offline critique” trains on Gemini critiques, and “w/o SFT” generates training critiques with the unadapted MOSS-Audio model. The full model combines SFT initialization with self-generated critiques. Arrows indicate the preferred direction, and bold marks the best value for each metric and dimension. \n583\tSelf-generated aesthetic critiques improve absolute-score agreement. \n584\t\n585\tRemoving the critiques increases macro-average MSE from 0.2316 to 0.5005, while the macro-averaged rank correlations remain close.\n586\tFigure 2 explains this difference: the no-critique model concentrates most predictions in a narrow range around 4.\n587\tIt can therefore preserve a coarse ranking among songs without recovering the dynamic range of expert ratings.\n588\tA reward model should preserve not only rank order but also the relative magnitude of quality differences through score intervals.\n589\tAn overly contracted prediction distribution reduces agreement with experts’ absolute ratings and weakens reward discrimination among samples of different quality during downstream policy optimization.\n590\tWith critiques, evaluative polarity and concrete musical descriptions provide an explicit semantic reference, bringing the prediction distribution closer to the expert scale and reducing MSE in every dimension. \n591\t\n592\tTraining on self-generated critiques improves reward prediction. \n593\t\n594\tTo isolate the role of the self-generated dataset 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} , we train a control model on 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} , using external-teacher critiques instead of critiques generated by the target model.\n595\tAs Table 4 shows, the full MuseCritic trained on 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} achieves higher LCC, SRCC, and KTAU than the offline-critique variant on every dimension.\n596\tReplacing 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} with 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} increases macro-average MSE from 0.2316 to 0.5481 and decreases macro-average LCC, SRCC, and KTAU from 0.9068, 0.8838, and 0.7178 to 0.8154, 0.7585, and 0.5685, respectively.\n597\tWe suggest that the external teacher and MOSS-Audio differ in vocabulary, information density, and patterns.\n598\tTraining only on teacher critiques therefore creates a larger mismatch between the critiques observed during reward training and those generated at inference time. \n599\t\n600\tSFT initializes effective critique generation. \n601\t\n602\tWithout SFT, we use the unadapted MOSS-Audio-8B-Instruct model to generate critiques and subsequently train the reward model on these critiques. This variant performs worst overall: macro-averaged MSE increases from 0.2316 to 0.7541, while macro-averaged LCC, SRCC, and KTAU decrease from 0.9068, 0.8838, and 0.7178 to 0.6695, 0.6660, and 0.5008, respectively. The degradation in both absolute-score accuracy and ranking consistency indicates that self-generation alone does not produce a reliable intermediate representation for reward learning. Supervised fine-tuning on teacher critiques is therefore necessary for the critique generator to acquire the evaluation criteria, domain-specific terminology, and rating semantics required to distinguish both the level and relative ordering of song quality. \n603\t\n604\t5 Conclusion \n605\t\n606\tWe introduced MuseCritic, a semi-scalar reward model for the aesthetic evaluation of complete songs.\n607\tThe model treats natural-language aesthetic critiques as intermediate variables: it first organizes evidence along five dimensions and then predicts continuous reward scores.\n608\tTraining initializes critique generation with external-teacher supervision and trains the reward head on self-generated critiques to mitigate the critique distribution shift between training and inference.\n609\tExperiments on SongEval, Music Arena, and three controlled ablations show that SFT-initialized, self-generated critiques reduce absolute scoring error while maintaining strong rank correlations.\n610\tUsing MuseCritic to train Muse with GRPO improves all nine metrics from two song-aesthetic evaluators, connecting critique-conditioned evaluation to preference optimization. \n611\t\n612\tLimitations \n613\t\n614\tThe critique-then-score procedure requires autoregressive critique generation before reward prediction and is therefore more computationally expensive than direct score regression.\n615\tMoreover, long-form song datasets that pair audio with expert aesthetic ratings remain scarce.\n616\tConsequently, MuseCritic is trained primarily on the Chinese and English vocal songs in SongEval.\n617\tFuture work can extend training to a broader range of languages and musical genres. \n618\t\n619\tReferences \n620\t\n621\tAgostinelli et al. (2023) A. Agostinelli, T. I. Denk, Z. Borsos, J. Engel, M. Verzetti, A. Caillon, Q. Huang, A. Jansen, A. Roberts, M. Tagliasacchi, M. Sharifi, N. Zeghidour, and C. Frank MusicLM: generating music from text . arXiv preprint arXiv:2301.11325 . External Links: Link Cited by: §1 , §2.1 . \n622\t\n623\tAnkner et al. (2024) Z. Ankner, M. Paul, B. Cui, J. D. Chang, and P. Ammanabrolu Critique-out-loud reward models . arXiv preprint arXiv:2408.11791 . External Links: Document , Link Cited by: §1 , §2.2 , §3.2 . \n624\t\n625\tBai et al. (2022) Y. Bai, S. Kadavath, S. Kundu, A. Askell, J. Kernion, A. Jones, A. Chen, A. Goldie, A. Mirhoseini, C. McKinnon, et al. Constitutional AI: harmlessness from AI feedback . arXiv preprint arXiv:2212.08073 . External Links: Document , Link Cited by: §3.2 . \n626\t\n627\tCideron et al. (2024) G. Cideron, S. Girgin, M. Verzetti, D. Vincent, M. Kastelic, Z. Borsos, B. McWilliams, V. Ungureanu, O. Bachem, O. Pietquin, M. Geist, L. Hussenot, N. Zeghidour, and A. Agostinelli MusicRL: aligning music generation to human preferences . arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.04229 . External Links: Link Cited by: §2.1 . \n628\t\n629\tCopet et al. (2023) J. Copet, F. Kreuk, I. Gat, T. Remez, D. Kant, G. Synnaeve, Y. Adi, and A. Défossez Simple and controllable music generation . In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems , External Links: Link Cited by: §1 , §2.1 . \n630\t\n631\tDeshmukh et al. (2024) S. Deshmukh, D. Alharthi, B. Elizalde, H. Gamper, M. A. Ismail, R. Singh, B. Raj, and H. Wang PAM: prompting audio-language models for audio quality assessment . arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.00282 . External Links: Document , Link Cited by: §1 , §2.2 . \n632\t\n633\tGong et al. (2025) J. Gong, S. Zhao, S. Wang, S. Xu, and J. Guo ACE-Step: a step towards music generation foundation