 Zhang, X. Zhang, H. Hao, Z. Guo, B. Yang, B. Zhang, Z. Ma, X. Wei, S. Bai, K. Chen, X. Liu, P. Wang, M. Yang, D. Liu, X. Ren, B. Zheng, R. Men, F. Zhou, B. Yu, J. Yang, L. Yu, J. Zhou, and J. Lin Qwen3-Omni technical report . arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.17765 . External Links: Link Cited by: §4.1 . \n662\t\n663\tYang et al. (2026) C. Yang, C. Yu, H. Chen, J. Zhu, J. Chen, K. Chen, W. Wang, Y. Wang, Y. Jiang, Y. Jiang, Z. Lin, Z. Chen, Z. Fei, C. Liu, D. Yu, J. Zhan, K. Yu, K. Huang, L. Fan, M. Chen, Q. Cheng, R. Li, S. Li, S. Wang, X. Zhao, Y. Gao, Y. Gong, Y. Zhang, Z. Xu, and X. Qiu MOSS-Audio technical report . arXiv preprint arXiv:2606.01802 . External Links: Link Cited by: §4.1 . \n664\t\n665\tYao et al. (2025) J. Yao, G. Ma, H. Xue, H. Chen, C. Hao, Y. Jiang, H. Liu, R. Yuan, J. Xu, W. Xue, H. Liu, and L. Xie SongEval: a benchmark dataset for song aesthetics evaluation . arXiv preprint arXiv:2505.10793 . External Links: Link Cited by: §1 , §2.2 , §3.1 , §4.1 , §4.1 , §4.1 . \n666\t\n667\tYu et al. (2025) Y. Yu, Z. Chen, A. Zhang, L. Tan, C. Zhu, R. Y. Pang, Y. Qian, X. Wang, S. Gururangan, C. Zhang, M. Kambadur, D. Mahajan, and R. Hou Self-generated critiques boost reward modeling for language models . In Proceedings of the 2025 Conference of the Nations of the Americas Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies (Volume 1: Long Papers) , pp. 11499–11514 . External Links: Document , Link Cited by: §1 , §2.2 . \n668\t\n669\tYuan et al. (2025) R. Yuan, H. Lin, S. Guo, G. Zhang, J. Pan, Y. Zang, H. Liu, Y. Liang, W. Ma, X. Du, et al. YuE: scaling open foundation models for long-form music generation . arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.08638 . External Links: Document , Link Cited by: §1 , §2.1 . \n670\t\n671\tZhang et al. (2024) L. Zhang, A. Hosseini, H. Bansal, M. Kazemi, A. Kumar, and R. Agarwal Generative verifiers: reward modeling as next-token prediction . arXiv preprint arXiv:2408.15240 . External Links: Document , Link Cited by: §1 , §2.2 . \n672\t\n673\tZheng et al. (2023) L. Zheng, W. Chiang, Y. Sheng, S. Zhuang, Z. Wu, Y. Zhuang, Z. Lin, Z. Li, D. Li, E. P. Xing, H. Zhang, J. E. Gonzalez, and I. Stoica Judging LLM-as-a-judge with MT-Bench and chatbot arena . arXiv preprint arXiv:2306.05685 . External Links: Document , Link Cited by: §1 , §2.2 . \n674\t\n675\tZhu et al. (2025) H. Zhu, Y. Zhou, H. Chen, J. Yu, Z. Ma, R. Gu, Y. Luo, W. Tan, and X. Chen MuQ: self-supervised music representation learning with mel residual vector quantization . arXiv preprint arXiv:2501.01108 . External Links: Document , Link Cited by: §1 , §2.2 . \n676\t\n677\tAppendix A Data Construction and Splitting \n678\t\n679\tSongEval split. \n680\t\n681\tWe load all 2,399 SongEval examples and shuffle them once using random seed 42. The first 2,199 examples form the training set, and the final 200 examples form the test set. We construct the split before generating critiques and reuse the same test examples across all main experiments, baselines, and ablation studies. Consequently, no test song appears in either the SFT data or the reward-learning data. \n682\t\n683\tOffline critique generation. \n684\t\n685\tThe original SongEval examples contain only song audio and five-dimensional mean expert ratings.\n686\tTo construct 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} , we provide Gemini-3-Pro with the song, definitions of the five dimensions, and the expert mean ratings.\n687\tThe prompt asks the model to infer perceptible musical evidence consistent with the scores on a five-point Likert scale, including melodic repetition and variation, section-level energy, rhythmic density, harmonic progression, vocal breathing, and changes in arrangement.\n688\tThe critique must neither state numerical scores nor invent the identity of a singer or band.\n689\tWe retain the generated aesthetic critique together with the original expert ratings. \n690\t\n691\tHuman verification of LLM-generated critiques. \n692\t\n693\tAfter critique generation and before SFT, a panel of eight music experts manually reviews the critiques generated by Gemini-3-Pro to determine whether they are consistent with human perceptual judgments of the corresponding songs.\n694\tFor each critique, the reviewers listen to the corresponding song and assess whether the commentary is grounded in audible musical evidence.\n695\tFactual accuracy and the absence of unsupported or hallucinated claims serve as the primary acceptance criteria. \n696\t\n697\tThe review additionally considers the depth of the analysis, whether its claims refer to perceptible musical phenomena, whether all five evaluation dimensions are adequately covered, and whether the critique is logically coherent and clearly written.\n698\tOnly critiques accepted through this verification process are retained as SFT data in 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} . \n699\t\n700\tSelf-generated critique construction. \n701\t\n702\tAfter the first SFT epoch, we run the resulting MOSS-Audio checkpoint over all 2,199 training songs.\n703\tWe use do_sample=False , num_beams=1 , and at most 4,096 new tokens, corresponding to greedy decoding with T = 0 T=0 .\n704\tWe replace each teacher critique with the corresponding self-generated critique while preserving the audio path, evaluation prompt, and expert mean ratings, thereby obtaining 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} . \n705\t\n706\tAppendix B Gemini Model Versions \n707\t\n708\tThe external teacher referred to as Gemini-3-Pro in the main text uses the model identifier gemini-3-pro-preview.\n709\tThe in-domain LLM-as-a-Judge baseline referred to as Gemini-3.1-Pro uses gemini-3.1-pro-preview. \n710\t\n711\tAppendix C Complete Training Configuration \n712\t\n713\tCritique supervised fine-tuning. \n714\t\n715\tWe fully fine-tune MOSS-Audio-8B-Instruct on 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} using four NVIDIA H200 GPUs, bfloat16 precision, a per-device batch size of 1, and gradient accumulation over 8 steps, giving a global batch size of 32.\n716\tThe learning rate is 5 × 10 − 5 5\\times 10^{-5} with no weight decay, a 5% warmup, cosine scheduling, a maximum sequence length of 10,000, and random seed 42.\n717\tAlthough the launch configuration specifies five epochs, we use checkpoint-69, saved after the first epoch, to initialize both the critic and the reward backbone; we therefore report one effective SFT epoch. \n718\t\n719\tMuseCritic reward learning. \n720\t\n721\tStarting from SFT checkpoint-69, we train MuseCritic for ten epochs on 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} using four NVIDIA H200 GPUs and bfloat16 precision.\n722\tThe backbone is updated with LoRA of rank 8 and scaling factor 32, while the reward head is fully trainable with dropout probability 0.1.\n723\tWe use a learning rate of 2 × 10 − 4 2\\times 10^{-4} , weight decay of 0.1, a per-device batch size of 1, gradient accumulation over 8 steps, a global batch size of 32, a 5% warmup, cosine scheduling, a maximum sequence length of 10,000, and random seed 42. \n724\t\n725\tSongEval baseline training. \n726\t\n727\tThe in-domain SongEval (UTMOS-based) baseline is fully trained for ten epochs on two NVIDIA H200 GPUs using bfloat16 precision, a learning rate of 1 × 10 − 4 1\\times 10^{-4} , weight decay of 0.1, a per-device batch size of 1, gradient accumulation over 8 steps, and a global batch size of 16.\n728\tIt uses the same 5% warmup, cosine scheduling, and random seed 42. \n729\t\n730\tReward-head implementation. \n731\t\n732\tThe final valid-token state from the last backbone layer first passes through dropout with probability 0.1, followed by a linear projection from ℝ d \\mathbb{R}^{d} to ℝ 5 \\mathbb{R}^{5} and a sigmoid range transformation that produces scores in ( 1 , 5 ) 5 (1,5)^{5} .\n733\tReward learning uses DeepSpeed ZeRO-3.\n734\tLoRA is applied to the language backbone, while the reward head remains fully trainable.\n735\tTraining uses only the MSE objective in Equation 10 , without an auxiliary critique language-modeling loss. \n736\t\n737\tCheckpoints and inference. \n738\t\n739\tWe save a checkpoint after every reward-learning epoch and use checkpoint-690 from the end of epoch ten for the main results.\n740\tDuring in-domain testing, the model generates at most 4,096 new tokens using greedy decoding with T = 0 T=0 .\n741\tThe five rewards are computed from the hidden state of the final valid token—the end-of-turn token following the critique—in the complete song–rubric–self-generated-critique sequence. \n742\t\n743\tAppendix D Aesthetic-Evaluation Prompt Templates \n744\t\n745\tFigure 3 presents the prompt used to construct 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} . At request time, the five variables in braces are replaced with the corresponding expert mean scores for the song, and the song audio is attached to the request. \n746\t\n747\tYou are an expert music critic.\n748\t\n749\tListen to a song and write a professional, detailed critique grounded in\n750\taudible musical phenomena while taking the human experts’ ratings as given.\n751\tReturn the response in Simplified Chinese.\n752\t\n753\tThe five evaluation dimensions are:\n754\t1. Overall Coherence\n755\t Evaluates musical and emotional continuity across the intro, verses,\n756\t choruses, and outro. High ratings reflect smooth transitions, consistent\n757\t dynamics, and a unified emotional tone throughout the piece.\n758\t2. Memorability\n759\t Evaluates distinctive features–such as a catchy melody, rhythmic motif,\n760\t or lyrical hook–that make the song easy to remember after one listen.\n761\t3. Naturalness of Vocal Breathing and Phrasing\n762\t Evaluates phrasing quality and breath control, including alignment with\n763\t semantic boundaries and rhythmic cues and whether breathing supports a\n764\t fluent delivery without disrupting the singing flow.\n765\t4. Clarity of Song Structure\n766\t Evaluates whether recognizable sections (e.g., verse, chorus, bridge) are\n767\t clearly delineated and logically organized. Conventional and novel forms\n768\t may both rate highly when their segmentation is musically meaningful.\n769\t5. Overall Musicality\n770\t Evaluates listening enjoyment based on melody, harmony, instrumentation,\n771\t and the integration of vocals and accompaniment.\n772\t\n773\tRequirements:\n774\t1. Use professional terminology from music theory and audio production.\n775\t2. Analyze each dimension separately. Begin each analysis with its number and\n776\t name, e.g., \"1. Overall Coherence: \".\n777\t3. Ground each analysis in concrete musical evidence consistent with the given\n778\t rating, such as melodic repetition/variation, section-level energy,\n779\t rhythmic density, harmonic progression, vocal breaths, or arrangement.\n780\t4. Do not reassess the ratings. Explain them through professional, specific,\n781\t and interpretable comments based on audible musical phenomena.\n782\t5. Do not state numerical ratings in the critique or say that a phenomenon\n783\t occurs \"because the rating is high/low.\"\n784\t6. Discuss only the song. Do not invent the singer’s or band’s identity.\n785\t7. The expert team’s ratings (1–5) are:\n786\t - Coherence: {coherence}\n787\t - Memorability: {memorability}\n788\t - Naturalness: {naturalness}\n789\t - Clarity: {clarity}\n790\t - Musicality: {musicality}\n791\t After the critique, reproduce these ratings in a JSON code block. The scores\n792\t are means across annotators and may therefore be non-integer values.\n793\t8. Ratings express agreement on a five-point Likert scale. Infer musical\n794\t evidence according to the following mapping:\n795\t - 5 (Excellent): outstanding quality with no discernible shortcomings.\n796\t - 4 (Good): strong quality with only minor room for improvement.\n797\t - 3 (Fair): adequate but unremarkable baseline quality.\n798\t - 2 (Poor): clear shortcomings that impair the overall effect.\n799\t - 1 (Very Poor): severe problems that fail to meet the criterion.\n800\t\n801\tOutput format:\n802\tReturn valid JSON following this format, without an introduction or summary.\n803\t{\n804\t \"critical\": \"Evaluate the song along all five dimensions…\",\n805\t \"scores\": {\n806\t \"coherence\": {coherence},\n807\t \"memorability\": {memorability},\n808\t \"naturalness\": {naturalness},\n809\t \"clarity\": {clarity},\n810\t \"musicality\": {musicality}\n811\t }\n812\t}\n813\t\n814\tNow evaluate the song. \n815\tFigure 3: Prompt used by Gemini-3-Pro to generate offline aesthetic critiques. At request time, the five variables in braces are replaced with the corresponding expert mean scores, and the audio is supplied concurrently. The expert scores constrain the evaluative polarity of the critique for each dimension, while the prompt explicitly prohibits numerical ratings in the critique text. \n816\t\n817\tFigure 4 presents the user prompt used for both the 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} and 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} training examples. This prompt is used for MuseCritic critique SFT, online critique generation, and subsequent reward modeling. The <audio> marker indicates the position at which the model processor inserts the song audio. \n818\t\n819\tYou are an expert music critic.\n820\t\n821\tListen to a song and write a professional, detailed critique in Simplified\n822\tChinese, grounded in audible musical phenomena.\n823\t\n824\tThe five evaluation dimensions are:\n825\t1. Overall Coherence\n826\t Evaluates musical and emotional continuity across the intro, verses,\n827\t choruses, and outro. High ratings reflect smooth transitions, consistent\n828\t dynamics, and a unified emotional tone throughout the piece.\n829\t2. Memorability\n830\t Evaluates distinctive features–such as a catchy melody, rhythmic motif,\n831\t or lyrical hook–that make the song easy to remember after one listen.\n832\t3. Naturalness of Vocal Breathing and Phrasing\n833\t Evaluates phrasing quality and breath control, including alignment with\n834\t semantic boundaries and rhythmic cues and whether breathing supports a\n835\t fluent delivery without disrupting the singing flow.\n836\t4. Clarity of Song Structure\n837\t Evaluates whether recognizable sections (e.g., verse, chorus, bridge) are\n838\t clearly delineated and logically organized. Conventional and novel forms\n839\t may both rate highly when their segmentation is musically meaningful.\n840\t5. Overall Musicality\n841\t Evaluates listening enjoyment based on melody, harmony, instrumentation,\n842\t and the integration of vocals and accompaniment.\n843\t\n844\tRequirements:\n845\t1. Use professional terminology from music theory and audio production.\n846\t2. Analyze each dimension separately. Begin each analysis with its number and\n847\t name, e.g., \"1. Overall Coherence: \".\n848\t3. Ground each dimension in concrete musical evidence, such as melodic\n849\t repetition/variation, section-level energy, rhythmic density, harmonic\n850\t progression, vocal breaths, or changes in arrangement.\n851\t4. Provide professional, specific, and interpretable comments based on audible\n852\t musical phenomena.\n853\t5. Discuss only the song. Do not invent the singer’s or band’s identity.\n854\t\n855\tNow evaluate the song.\n856\t<audio> \n857\tFigure 4: Prompt used to train the SFT-Critique-Generator, generate online aesthetic critiques with MOSS-Audio, and perform MuseCritic reward-model training. The model generates critiques along five dimensions without receiving expert scores; <audio> indicates the audio input position. \n858\t\n859\tAudiobox Aesthetics ↑ \\uparrow \n860\tSongEval ↑ \\uparrow \n861\t\n862\tWindow \n863\tModel \n864\tCE \n865\tCU \n866\tPC \n867\tPQ \n868\tCO \n869\tMU \n870\tME \n871\tCL \n872\tNA \n873\t\n874\tFull audio \n875\tMuse \n876\t6.79 \n877\t7.50 \n878\t5.77 \n879\t7.93 \n880\t3.76 \n881\t3.56 \n882\t3.72 \n883\t3.65 \n884\t3.62 \n885\t\n886\tMuse-GRPO \n887\t7.03 \n888\t7.55 \n889\t6.05 \n890\t7.98 \n891\t3.89 \n892\t3.68 \n893\t3.84 \n894\t3.77 \n895\t3.73 \n896\t\n897\t360 s \n898\tMuse \n899\t6.93 \n900\t7.53 \n901\t5.92 \n902\t7.97 \n903\t3.85 \n904\t3.65 \n905\t3.81 \n906\t3.73 \n907\t3.70 \n908\t\n909\tMuse-GRPO \n910\t7.10 \n911\t7.56 \n912\t6.13 \n913\t8.00 \n914\t3.94 \n915\t3.73 \n916\t3.89 \n917\t3.82 \n918\t3.77 \n919\t\n920\t330 s \n921\tMuse \n922\t7.00 \n923\t7.55 \n924\t6.00 \n925\t7.99 \n926\t3.88 \n927\t3.69 \n928\t3.83 \n929\t3.76 \n930\t3.72 \n931\t\n932\tMuse-GRPO \n933\t7.16 \n934\t7.58 \n935\t6.19 \n936\t8.02 \n937\t3.96 \n938\t3.76 \n939\t3.91 \n940\t3.84 \n941\t3.79 \n942\t\n943\t300 s \n944\tMuse \n945\t7.10 \n946\t7.58 \n947\t6.11 \n948\t8.03 \n949\t3.92 \n950\t3.73 \n951\t3.87 \n952\t3.80 \n953\t3.76 \n954\t\n955\tMuse-GRPO \n956\t7.24 \n957\t7.60 \n958\t6.29 \n959\t8.04 \n960\t3.99 \n961\t3.80 \n962\t3.94 \n963\t3.87 \n964\t3.82 \n965\t\n966\t270 s \n967\tMuse \n968\t7.21 \n969\t7.60 \n970\t6.24 \n971\t8.06 \n972\t3.96 \n973\t3.78 \n974\t3.90 \n975\t3.84 \n976\t3.79 \n977\t\n978\tMuse-GRPO \n979\t7.34 \n980\t7.63 \n981\t6.40 \n982\t8.08 \n983\t4.03 \n984\t3.84 \n985\t3.97 \n986\t3.91 \n987\t3.85 \n988\t\n989\t240 s \n990\tMuse \n991\t7.33 \n992\t7.64 \n993\t6.37 \n994\t8.11 \n995\t4.01 \n996\t3.84 \n997\t3.94 \n998\t3.89 \n999\t3.83 \n1000\t\n1001\tMuse-GRPO \n1002\t7.45 \n1003\t7.66 \n1004\t6.52 \n1005\t8.12 \n1006\t4.07 \n1007\t3.89 \n1008\t4.00 \n1009\t3.94 \n1010\t3.89 \n1011\tTable 5: Robustness of downstream aesthetic evaluation across audio windows. Both models are evaluated on the same 100 Muse test prompts. “Full audio” uses each complete generation; the remaining rows apply a common maximum-duration prefix, leaving shorter songs unchanged. Metric abbreviations follow Table 3 . Bold marks the higher observed value within each window. \n1012\t\n1013\tAppendix E Music Arena Evaluation Details \n1014\t\n1015\tEach Music Arena example contains a chosen and rejected song generated under the same text condition.\n1016\tFor MuseCritic and SongEval with the official MuQ-based weights, we obtain five-dimensional scores for each song and take their arithmetic mean.\n1017\tFor Audiobox Aesthetics, we average its four aesthetic dimensions.\n1018\tFor Qwen3-Omni, we parse and average its five generated ratings.\n1019\tAn example is correct only if r ⁡ ( x + ) > r ⁡ ( x − ) r(x^{+})>r(x^{-}) ; ties count as errors.\n1020\tFinal accuracy is the fraction of correct predictions across all 733 preference pairs. \n1021\t\n1022\tAppendix F Muse-GRPO Training and Evaluation Details \n1023\t\n1024\tTraining data. \n1025\t\n1026\tWe sample 500 multi-turn song-generation examples from the public Muse training data with seed 42 while preserving the original language distribution: 305 English and 195 Chinese examples.\n1027\tAll inputs retain the Muse multi-turn prompt format, and the policy is initialized from Muse-0.6B. \n1028\t\n1029\tGRPO configuration. \n1030\t\n1031\tTraining uses six NVIDIA H200 GPUs, a per-device batch size of 2, gradient accumulation over 2 steps, eight candidate generations, a learning rate of 10 − 6 10^{-6} , a 5% warmup, and one epoch.\n1032\tThe vLLM rollout server uses two NVIDIA H200 GPUs, a maximum model length of 20,000 and GPU memory utilization of 0.7.\n1033\tSampling uses temperature 0.9, top- p p 0.9, and a repetition penalty of 1.3.\n1034\tEach completion contains at most 3,000 tokens, and a complete multi-turn trajectory contains at most 20,000 tokens.\n1035\tThe reward for each candidate song is the mean of the five MuseCritic scores; within-group relative advantages are computed according to GRPO. \n1036\t\n1037\tEvaluation protocol. \n1038\t\n1039\tWe evaluate on the 100 multi-turn test prompts from Muse ( 12 ) and apply identical greedy decoding with T = 0 T=0 to both the original Muse and the trained Muse-GRPO .\n1040\tUnder modality-extended generation, deterministic decoding can exhibit inference-time instability on some evaluation examples: the model may fail to produce a valid ending and instead enter token-level repetition, producing an extended noisy tail.\n1041\tThis behavior does not consistently occur on a fixed subset of the evaluation prompts.\n1042\tTo prevent this decoding artifact from dominating the aesthetic assessment of otherwise valid song content while retaining all 100 test examples, we adopt a fixed-prefix protocol.\n1043\tAll generations longer than 240 seconds are evaluated using their first 240 seconds, whereas shorter generations remain unchanged and are neither padded nor looped.\n1044\tThe same deterministic preprocessing is applied to Muse and Muse-GRPO .\n1045\tWe then compute the nine metrics in Table 3 with Audiobox Aesthetics and SongEval, focusing the main comparison on the aesthetic quality of the valid musical content. \n1046\t\n1047\tRobustness across evaluation windows. \n1048\t\n1049\tAs a robustness check, we evaluate both complete, untruncated generations and fixed-prefix windows of 240, 270, 300, 330, and 360 seconds.\n1050\tTable 5 shows that Muse-GRPO obtains higher observed values than Muse on all nine metrics at every evaluation window, including the complete audio.\n1051\tThe 240-second protocol reduces the observed Muse– Muse-GRPO gap on every metric relative to the untruncated evaluation, indicating that the main results do not depend on excluding the noisy tails and provide a conservative estimate of the relative improvement. \n1052\t\n1053\tAppendix G Reproducibility Statement \n1054\t\n1055\tWe use random seed 42 for data splitting, model training, and sampling.\n1056\tOnline critique generation, in-domain testing, Music Arena comparisons, and song generation for the 100 downstream prompts all use deterministic decoding to eliminate sampling variance in model comparisons. The fixed-prefix protocol described in Appendix F is used to handle occasional degenerate tails in downstream generations. \n1057\t\n1058\tAppendix H Ethics Statement \n1059\t\n1060\tThis study follows applicable data-privacy regulations and ethical standards in data acquisition and experimental evaluation.\n1061\tBoth the training and evaluation sets are constructed from public datasets, and the study uses no private personal data.\n1062\tGiven the data types and scope of this work, we identify no additional substantial ethical risks. \n1063\t\n1064\tAppendix I AI Assistants in Research or Writing \n1065\t\n1066\tDuring the preparation of this manuscript, AI assistive technologies were utilized exclusively to enhance linguistic clarity, readability, and stylistic presentation. Specifically, these tools were employed to polish phrasing and refine the text. AI played no role in the research conceptualization, methodology design, data collection, data analysis, interpretation of results, or the formulation of scientific conclusions. The authors assume full responsibility for all intellectual contributions and the final content of this work. \n1067\t\n1068\tMajor funding support from \n1069\t\n1070\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f84f91a3-634e-4bd2-96e5-a713d5ca7ef5", "uuid": "d01b2f5f-00cd-478c-9bdb-f2f4cb2f3e31", "timestamp": "2026-08-13T14:23:36.131Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/musecritic_text.txt", "content": "MuseCritic: Learning Multi-Aspect Song Rewards through Natural-Language Aesthetic Critiques \nTitle: \nContent selection saved. Describe the issue below: \nDescription: \n\narXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × \n\nLicense: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license \narXiv:2608.11755v1 [cs.SD] 12 Aug 2026 \n\n† † * Equal contribution. † Corresponding authors \nMuseCritic: Learning Multi-Aspect Song Rewards through Natural-Language Aesthetic Critiques \n\nJiabao Zhuang *,† , Changhao Jiang * , Hanchen Wang * , \nJiahao Chen * , Zhixiong Yang * , Zhenghao Xiang * , \nYifei Cao,\nJiajun Sun,\nHui Li,\nMing Zhang, \nTao Ji,\nTao Gui † ,\nQi Zhang,\nXuanjing Huang \nFudan NLP Group, Fudan University \n25213050071@m.fudan.edu.cn, tgui@fudan.edu.cn \n\nAbstract \nLong-form song generation models continue to improve in duration, structural integrity, and acoustic complexity, making reliable aesthetic rewards increasingly important for aligning these models with human preferences. However, reward models for complete songs remain limited, and existing evaluators typically predict scores in a single forward pass without providing readable explanations. To this end, we introduce MuseCritic, a semi-scalar reward model that generates a natural-language critique covering five aesthetic dimensions and uses it as an intermediate representation to predict continuous reward scores. MuseCritic follows a two-stage training pipeline: a teacher model first provides high-quality critiques for supervised fine-tuning, after which the fine-tuned model generates its own critiques for reward learning, mitigating distribution shift between training and inference. On an in-domain test set of 200 SongEval songs, MuseCritic reduces macro-averaged mean squared error from 0.2875 to 0.2316 and improves macro-averaged LCC, SRCC, and Kendall’s τ \\tau to 0.9068, 0.8838, and 0.7178, respectively. On the out-of-domain Music Arena benchmark with 733 preference pairs, it achieves the highest accuracy of 71.35%. Moreover, using MuseCritic with GRPO improves Muse-0.6B on all nine aesthetic metrics from SongEval and Audiobox Aesthetics. These results demonstrate that critique-conditioned reward modeling reduces scoring error and provides an effective optimization signal for song generation. The project repository is available at https://github.com/WuqnEl/MuseCritic . \n\n1 Introduction \n\nText-to-music generation has progressed from synthesizing short clips to composing complete songs. Early systems generate musical excerpts from natural-language descriptions ( 1 ; 5 ) , while recent systems—including DiffRhythm, YuE, LeVo, ACE-Step, and Muse—support minutes-long vocal music, lyric alignment, and fine-grained style control ( 17 ; 25 ; 14 ; 7 ; 12 ) . As generation length and acoustic realism improve, reliable aesthetic evaluation becomes increasingly important for model selection and for providing optimization signals aligned with human preferences. \n\nExisting evaluators, however, remain limited for complete songs. Objective metrics such as phoneme error rate, MuLan, and MuQ-MuLan capture lyric intelligibility or audio–text correspondence rather than perceptual and artistic quality ( 11 ; 28 ) . PAM, MusicEval, and Audiobox Aesthetics broaden evaluation to audio quality and musical aesthetics ( 6 ; 15 ; 20 ) , while SongEval provides five expert aesthetic criteria for full-length synthetic songs ( 23 ) ; nevertheless, these evaluators map audio directly to scores and do not explain the audible evidence behind their judgments. In contrast, LLM-as-a-Judge and critique-conditioned reward models show that language models can interpret rubrics and generate corresponding rationales or supporting reasoning before scoring textual responses, thereby improving textual preference modeling ( 27 ; 2 ; 24 ; 26 ; 16 ) . Whether this linguistic intermediate representation can improve multidimensional aesthetic reward modeling for complete songs remains underexplored. Existing audio reward models predict scores directly, while general-purpose multimodal models lack task-specific training and show weaker agreement with expert judgments. \n\nFigure 1: Overview of the two-stage training and inference pipeline of MuseCritic. In Stage I, Gemini-3-Pro generates aesthetic critiques from songs, evaluation rubrics, and expert mean ratings to construct 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} , which is used to fine-tune a pretrained large language model into an SFT critique generator. In Stage II, the SFT model replaces the teacher critiques with one self-generated five-aspect critique per song to construct 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} ; a scalar reward head is then initialized, and MuseCritic is trained using expert mean ratings as regression targets. At inference time, MuseCritic first generates a five-aspect critique and then predicts five continuous aesthetic scores conditioned on the song, rubric, and self-generated critique. \n\nIn this work, we investigate explicit linguistic reasoning in song reward modeling, enabling the model to form a representation of aesthetic evidence aligned with the evaluation rubric before predicting multidimensional scores. To this end, we propose MuseCritic, a semi-scalar reward model built around natural-language aesthetic critiques. Given a song and a five-dimensional evaluation rubric, MuseCritic generates a single critique containing five labeled analyses: Overall Coherence, Memorability, Naturalness of Vocal Breathing and Phrasing, Clarity of Song Structure, and Overall Musicality. Conditioned on the song, rubric, and self-generated critique, a reward head then predicts five continuous aesthetic scores ranging from 1 to 5. The critique is not a post-hoc text appended to predicted scores; it is an explicit intermediate variable connecting song representations to rewards. This design introduces a variable-length textual representation before prediction and organizes auditory evidence distributed across a long song into dimension-aligned descriptions. \n\nFigure 1 summarizes the training and inference pipeline. The model first learns five-dimensional aesthetic analysis from critiques generated by an external teacher. It then generates self-generated critiques for the training songs and performs reward learning on the resulting dataset. \n\nAcross both in-domain and out-of-domain evaluations, critique-conditioned reward modeling consistently improves agreement with human judgments. On the held-out SongEval test set, MuseCritic reduces macro-averaged MSE from 0.2875 to 0.2316 and raises macro-averaged LCC, SRCC, and Kendall’s τ \\tau to 0.9068, 0.8838, and 0.7178. On Music Arena, MuseCritic attains the highest observed pairwise accuracy of 71.35% across 733 preference pairs. When used as the reward model for GRPO, it further improves Muse-0.6B on all nine aesthetic metrics reported by SongEval and Audiobox Aesthetics. \n\nOur contributions are threefold: \n\n• \n\nWe introduce MuseCritic, a semi-scalar reward model for complete songs. It generates a natural-language critique containing five dimension-specific analyses and uses this critique to predict five continuous scores, combining verbal evaluation and reward estimation within a single model (Figure 1 ). \n\n• \n\nWe systematically evaluate MuseCritic on in-domain SongEval ratings and out-of-domain Music Arena preferences. Self-generated critiques improve agreement with expert scores and mitigate the critique distribution shift between reward training and inference (Tables 1 , 2 , and 4 ). \n\n• \n\nWe use MuseCritic as the reward model for GRPO training of Muse-0.6B. The resulting policy improves on all nine observed metrics from SongEval and Audiobox Aesthetics, showing that the learned reward provides a useful optimization signal (Table 3 ). \n\n2 Related Work \n\n2.1 Text-to-Song Generation \n\nAdvances in neural audio codecs and large-scale audio generation have moved language-conditioned music synthesis from early systems such as MusicLM and MusicGen toward recent systems capable of generating complete songs ( 1 ; 5 ) .\nUnlike short musical excerpts, complete songs require cross-section structure, coordination between vocals and accompaniment, and long-range lyric alignment. \n\nRecent systems can generate complete songs several minutes in duration.\nDiffRhythm performs end-to-end long-form song synthesis through latent diffusion ( 17 ) ; YuE combines dual-track tokenization with progressive structural conditioning to generate songs up to five minutes long ( 25 ) ; and ACE-Step integrates diffusion with musical semantic representations ( 7 ) .\nLeVo and Muse further support vocal–accompaniment coordination and fine-grained, multi-turn style control ( 14 ; 12 ) .\nMusicRL learns music rewards from human feedback ( 4 ) , while LeVo uses multi-preference DPO to improve song quality and instruction following ( 14 ) .\nAs complete-song generation matures, reliable aesthetic evaluation and reward signals suitable for reinforcement learning become central challenges.\nOur work focuses on the reward itself, enabling one model to both describe five-dimensional aesthetic quality and assign continuous rewards. \n\n2.2 Reward Modeling and Music Aesthetic Evaluation \n\nClassical RLHF reward models attach a shallow scalar head to a pretrained language-model backbone to fit human preferences ( 18 ) , whereas LLM-as-a-Judge uses capable language models to produce evaluations and supporting rationales ( 27 ) .\nSemi-scalar and generative reward models seek to combine linguistic explanation with numerical prediction.\nCLoud generates natural-language critiques before predicting rewards ( 2 ) ; Critic-RM jointly trains self-generated critiques and reward prediction ( 24 ) ; and Generative Verifiers and DeepSeek-GRM improve reward judgments through reasoning traces and test-time sampling ( 26 ; 16 ) .\nThese approaches primarily evaluate text responses.\nMuseCritic extends this modeling paradigm to long-form song audio and multidimensional continuous aesthetic assessment. \n\nExisting music evaluators typically predict proxy metrics or scalar scores.\nMuLan and MuQ-MuLan measure text–music correspondence ( 11 ; 28 ) ; PAM estimates reference-free audio quality ( 6 ) ; MusicEval trains a CLAP-based evaluator on expert ratings of generated music clips ( 15 ) ; and Audiobox Aesthetics predicts content enjoyment, content usefulness, production complexity, and production quality ( 20 ) .\nSongEval defines five dimensions for complete Chinese and English songs: coherence, memorability, vocal naturalness, structural clarity, and musicality ( 23 ) , but still targets score prediction alone.\nMuseCritic adopts the SongEval rubric while retaining the critique-generation capability of an audio language model, allowing explicit aesthetic evidence to participate in reward prediction. \n\n3 Method \n\n3.1 Preliminaries \n\nProblem formulation. \n\nLet x ∈ 𝒳 x\\in\\mathcal{X} denote a complete song with vocals, p p an aesthetic-evaluation prompt that defines the assessment dimensions, and K = 5 K=5 the number of dimensions.\nThe expert annotation is 𝐲 ∗ = [ y 1 ∗ , … , y K ∗ ] ∈ [ 1 , 5 ] K \\mathbf{y}^{*}=[y^{*}_{1},\\ldots,y^{*}_{K}]\\in[1,5]^{K} .\nThe five dimensions are Overall Coherence, Memorability, Naturalness of Vocal Breathing and Phrasing, Clarity of Song Structure, and Overall Musicality ( 23 ) .\nWe use an instruction-tuned audio language model f θ f_{\\theta} as the shared backbone of our semi-scalar reward model.\nIts critique generator g c g_{c} and aesthetic scorer g s g_{s} are \n\ng c = h LM ∘ f θ , g s = h RM ∘ f θ , g_{c}=h_{\\mathrm{LM}}\\circ f_{\\theta},\\qquad g_{s}=h_{\\mathrm{RM}}\\circ f_{\\theta}, \n\n(1) \n\nwhere h LM h_{\\mathrm{LM}} is a language-modeling head that generates a natural-language aesthetic critique, and h RM h_{\\mathrm{RM}} is a reward-modeling head that produces K K continuous aesthetic scores. The two components always share the same backbone parameters θ \\theta . \n\nReward modeling. \n\nUnlike reward models that predict a scalar directly from audio, the aesthetic scorer of MuseCritic jointly conditions on the song x x , evaluation rubric p p , and aesthetic critique c c : \n\n𝐲 ^ = g s ​ ( x , p , c ) . \\hat{\\mathbf{y}}=g_{s}(x,p,c). \n\n(2) \n\nFor any set 𝒮 \\mathcal{S} of expert-annotated samples, we learn the five-dimensional scores by minimizing mean squared error: \n\nℒ MSE ​ ( 𝒮 , g s ) \\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{MSE}}(\\mathcal{S};g_{s}) \n= 1 | 𝒮 | ​ K ∑ ( x , p , c , 𝐲 ∗ ) ∈ 𝒮 \\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{S}|K}\\sum_{(x,p,c,\\mathbf{y}^{*})\\in\\mathcal{S}} \n\n(3) \n\n‖ g s ​ ( x , p , c ) − 𝐲 ∗ ‖ 2 2 . \\displaystyle\\left\\|g_{s}(x,p,c)-\\mathbf{y}^{*}\\right\\|_{2}^{2}. \n\nThis objective simultaneously constrains all five dimensions to align the predictions with expert ratings. \n\n3.2 The MuseCritic Reward Model \n\nOverview. \n\nTo introduce an explicit textual intermediate representation into reward modeling, we treat natural-language aesthetic critiques as intermediate variables between a song and its final scores.\nGiven a song x x and rubric p p , MuseCritic first generates \n\nc ^ = g c ​ ( x , p ) , \\hat{c}=g_{c}(x,p), \n\n(4) \n\nwhere c ^ \\hat{c} is a single critique with five labeled sections describing perceptible musical phenomena under the corresponding dimensions.\nThe shared backbone then receives the song, rubric, and self-generated critique to produce \n\n𝐲 ^ = g s ​ ( x , p , c ^ ) . \\hat{\\mathbf{y}}=g_{s}(x,p,\\hat{c}). \n\n(5) \n\nRelative to direct score prediction, Equation 5 introduces a variable-length textual representation before scoring and organizes descriptions of auditory evidence distributed across a long song according to the evaluation rubric. \n\nTraining MuseCritic. \n\nIdeally, MuseCritic would be trained on a dataset 𝒟 ∗ = { ( x i , p , c i ∗ , 𝐲 i ∗ ) } i = 1 N \\mathcal{D}^{*}=\\{(x_{i},p,c_{i}^{*},\\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*})\\}_{i=1}^{N} , where c i ∗ c_{i}^{*} is a human-authored aesthetic critique consistent with the corresponding expert ratings.\nSuch critiques would be authored by human music experts, but existing song datasets rarely pair expert scores with expert critiques.\nFollowing prior work that constructs supervision from model feedback ( 3 ; 2 ) , we use the audio-capable Gemini-3-Pro ( 9 ) to generate score-conditioned synthetic critiques.\nSpecifically, we begin with a score-only dataset 𝒟 score = { ( x i , 𝐲 i ∗ ) } i = 1 N \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{score}}=\\{(x_{i},\\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*})\\}_{i=1}^{N} .\nTo generate a critique, Gemini observes the song x i x_{i} , rubric p p , and expert mean ratings 𝐲 i ∗ \\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*} , then writes dimension-specific commentary whose evaluative polarity is consistent with each rating on a five-point Likert scale.\nThe prompt requests analysis of concrete musical phenomena while prohibiting numerical scores in the critique itself.\nThis procedure yields the offline critique dataset \n\n𝒟 off = { ( x i , p , c i off , 𝐲 i ∗ ) } i = 1 N . \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}}=\\{(x_{i},p,c_{i}^{\\mathrm{off}},\\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*})\\}_{i=1}^{N}. \n\n(6) \n\nAppendix D provides the complete external-teacher prompt.\nTo further ensure the reliability of the aesthetic critiques, we manually verify the generated critiques, as detailed in Appendix A . \n\nTraining comprises two stages.\nIn Stage I, we supervise the audio language model on 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} with the critique-generation objective \n\nℒ SFT = \\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{SFT}}={} \n− 1 ∑ i = 1 N | c i off | \\displaystyle-\\frac{1}{\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\lvert c_{i}^{\\mathrm{off}}\\rvert} \n\n(7) \n\n∑ i = 1 N ∑ t = 1 | c i off | log ⁡ p θ ​ ( c i , t off ∣ x i , p , c i , < t off ) . \\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\sum_{t=1}^{\\lvert c_{i}^{\\mathrm{off}}\\rvert}\\log p_{\\theta}\\!\\left(c_{i,t}^{\\mathrm{off}}\\mid x_{i},p,c_{i,<t}^{\\mathrm{off}}\\right). \n\nThis is the standard next-token cross-entropy averaged over non-masked critique target tokens; the audio and user-prompt tokens do not contribute to the loss.\nThis stage produces a critique generator g c ′ g^{\\prime}_{c} with the ability to analyze a song along the five aesthetic dimensions. \n\nIn Stage II, we first use g c ′ g^{\\prime}_{c} to generate a new critique for every training example: \n\nc i on = g c ′ ​ ( x i , p ) . c_{i}^{\\mathrm{on}}=g^{\\prime}_{c}(x_{i},p). \n\n(8) \n\nWe replace the external-teacher critiques with these self-generated critiques and call the resulting collection the self-generated critique dataset 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} : \n\n𝒟 on = { ( x i , p , c i on , 𝐲 i ∗ ) } i = 1 N . \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}}=\\{(x_{i},p,c_{i}^{\\mathrm{on}},\\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*})\\}_{i=1}^{N}. \n\n(9) \n\nWe then initialize the shared MuseCritic backbone from the SFT model and jointly update the backbone LoRA parameters and all reward-head parameters on 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} .\nThe reward model thus learns from critiques generated by the target model family rather than by the external teacher, mitigating the critique distribution shift between reward training and inference.\nThe objective is the MSE from Equation 3 applied to 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} : \n\nℒ MC = ℒ MSE ​ ( 𝒟 on , g s ) . \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{MC}}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{MSE}}(\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}};g_{s}). \n\n(10) \n\nAt inference time, the final LoRA-updated MuseCritic backbone generates an aesthetic critique according to Equation 4 and then predicts five rewards according to Equation 5 . \n\n4 Experiments \n\n4.1 Experimental Setup \n\nData. \n\nWe use SongEval ( 23 ) for training and in-domain evaluation.\nIt contains 2,399 complete Chinese and English songs totaling more than 140 hours of audio, with five-dimensional aesthetic scores from 16 annotators with music expertise.\nThe data span nine major genres, including pop, rock, and electronic music.\nBecause the original work does not release a fixed train–test split, we shuffle all samples with seed 42, use 2,199 songs for training, and reserve the remaining 200 for testing.\nEvery experiment uses this split.\nFor the training set, Gemini-3-Pro ( 9 ) observes each song and its expert mean ratings to generate a single critique containing five dimension-specific analyses, producing 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} .\nThe SFT model subsequently regenerates one five-part critique per song with greedy decoding ( T = 0 T=0 , no sampling), producing the self-generated critique dataset 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} .\nAppendices D and A provide the teacher and model prompts and the splitting algorithm. \n\nFor out-of-domain evaluation, we use the 733 song-preference pairs from Music Arena ( 13 ) .\nThis dataset contains outputs from systems not represented in SongEval and provides pairwise human preferences rather than five-dimensional absolute scores.\nIt therefore measures preference generalization under distribution shift. \n\nBaselines. \n\nThe in-domain task requires five-dimensional song aesthetic scores.\nWe compare against two classes of baselines: (1) Gemini-3.1-Pro ( 8 ) , an audio-capable LLM-as-a-Judge that generates critiques and five-dimensional ratings under the same rubric; and (2) SongEval (UTMOS) , a discriminative reward model that directly regresses five scores from audio.\nSongEval does not release its original training code or data split, so we reimplement its published method and UTMOS architecture and train it for ten epochs on our 2,199/200 split, controlling for differences in training data. \n\nMusic Arena requires pairwise preference prediction.\nIn addition to MuseCritic, we evaluate the officially released SongEval weights ( 23 ) , the general-purpose audio aesthetic model Audiobox Aesthetics ( 20 ) , and the open-source audio language model Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B-Instruct ( 21 ) .\nQwen3-Omni uses the same rubric and generative scoring format as the in-domain Gemini baseline. \n\nMetrics. \n\nFor in-domain evaluation, we report dimension-wise mean squared error (MSE), Pearson’s linear correlation coefficient (LCC), Spearman’s rank correlation coefficient (SRCC), and Kendall’s τ \\tau (KTAU).\nMSE measures agreement with the experts’ absolute rating scale, while the three correlation coefficients measure relative agreement across songs in terms of linear association and ranking.\nEach metric is computed independently over the 200 test songs for each dimension. \n\nFor out-of-domain evaluation, we report pairwise accuracy.\nA prediction is correct only when the model assigns a strictly higher overall reward to the human-preferred song; ties count as errors.\nFor SongEval, Audiobox Aesthetics, and MuseCritic, we average the respective output dimensions with equal weight.\nFor Qwen3-Omni, we parse its five generated ratings under the shared rubric and take their mean.\nAll main evaluations use deterministic decoding with T = 0 T=0 . \n\nFor downstream evaluation after GRPO, music quality is assessed using SongEval ( 23 ) in terms of overall coherence (CO), memorability (ME), naturalness of vocal breathing and phrasing (NA), clarity of song structure (CL), and overall musicality (MU).\nAudio quality is evaluated with Audiobox Aesthetics ( 20 ) , which considers content enjoyment (CE), content usefulness (CU), production complexity (PC), and production quality (PQ). \n\nTraining. \n\nAll MuseCritic variants use MOSS-Audio-8B-Instruct ( 22 ) as the base model.\nDuring critique cold-start, we fine-tune all backbone parameters with a learning rate of 5 × 10 − 5 5\\times 10^{-5} and use the checkpoint saved after one epoch.\nWe initialize MuseCritic from this checkpoint and train it for ten epochs on the self-generated critique data.\nThe backbone uses LoRA (rank 8, scaling factor 32) ( 10 ) , while the reward head is fully updated with a learning rate of 2 × 10 − 4 2\\times 10^{-4} .\nBoth optimization stages use four NVIDIA H200 GPUs, a per-device batch size of 1, gradient accumulation over 8 steps (global batch size 32), a 5% warmup, cosine learning-rate scheduling, and a fixed random seed of 42.\nReward learning uses a weight decay of 0.1.\nThe offline-critique and no-critique ablations use exactly the same reward-learning configuration.\nAppendix C lists all hyperparameters. \n\n4.2 Evaluation Results \n\nDimension \nModel \nMSE ↓ \\downarrow \nLCC ↑ \\uparrow \nSRCC ↑ \\uparrow \nKTAU ↑ \\uparrow \n\nCoherence \nMuseCritic \n0.2058 \n0.9147 \n0.8894 \n0.7262 \n\nGemini-3.1-Pro \n0.9881 \n0.5906 \n0.5898 \n0.4359 \n\nSongEval (UTMOS) \n0.2605 \n0.8876 \n0.8605 \n0.6835 \n\nMemorability \nMuseCritic \n0.2511 \n0.9098 \n0.8839 \n0.7232 \n\nGemini-3.1-Pro \n1.1183 \n0.5806 \n0.5623 \n0.4113 \n\nSongEval (UTMOS) \n0.3055 \n0.8857 \n0.8595 \n0.6837 \n\nVocal naturalness \nMuseCritic \n0.2537 \n0.8906 \n0.8678 \n0.6945 \n\nGemini-3.1-Pro \n1.9736 \n0.4921 \n0.5237 \n0.3877 \n\nSongEval (UTMOS) \n0.3071 \n0.8593 \n0.8293 \n0.6491 \n\nStructural clarity \nMuseCritic \n0.2275 \n0.9068 \n0.8806 \n0.7109 \n\nGemini-3.1-Pro \n1.4740 \n0.5532 \n0.5752 \n0.4162 \n\nSongEval (UTMOS) \n0.2730 \n0.8831 \n0.8565 \n0.6790 \n\nMusicality \nMuseCritic \n0.2202 \n0.9120 \n0.8972 \n0.7344 \n\nGemini-3.1-Pro \n0.9763 \n0.6152 \n0.6099 \n0.4459 \n\nSongEval (UTMOS) \n0.2913 \n0.8807 \n0.8599 \n0.6876 \nTable 1: In-domain five-dimensional aesthetic scoring on the SongEval test set. All models are evaluated on the fixed test set of 200 songs. SongEval (UTMOS) denotes the regressor retrained on the same 2,199/200 split. MSE measures absolute error against expert ratings; LCC, SRCC, and KTAU denote Pearson, Spearman, and Kendall τ \\tau correlations. Arrows indicate the preferred direction, and bold marks the best value for each metric and dimension. \n\nModel \nAccuracy (%) ↑ \\uparrow \n\nQwen3-Omni-30B-A3B \n53.75 \n\nAudiobox Aesthetics \n68.49 \n\nSongEval \n70.80 \n\nMuseCritic \n71.35 \nTable 2: Out-of-domain pairwise preference results on Music Arena. The test set contains 733 song pairs with human preference labels. A prediction is correct when the model assigns a higher mean aesthetic score to the human-preferred song; ties count as errors. SongEval uses its officially released weights. MuseCritic achieves the highest observed accuracy. \n\n4.2.1 In-Domain Song Aesthetic Scoring \n\nTable 1 compares model predictions with the expert mean ratings of the 200-song held-out SongEval test set.\nMuseCritic achieves the lowest MSE and highest correlation on all five dimensions.\nRelative to the SongEval (UTMOS) regression baseline trained on the same data, MuseCritic reduces macro-averaged MSE from 0.2875 to 0.2316.\nMacro-averaged LCC, SRCC, and KTAU increase from 0.8793, 0.8531, and 0.6766 to 0.9068, 0.8838, and 0.7178.\nSection 4.4 isolates the effect of critique modeling through controlled ablations. \n\nAlthough Gemini-3.1-Pro has not been specialized for song aesthetics, it generates coherent critiques.\nIts macro-average MSE of 1.3061, however, substantially exceeds the 0.2316 of MuseCritic, and its dimension-wise correlations fall below those of models supervised by expert scores.\nThis result indicates that an unadapted LLM-as-a-Judge cannot reliably reproduce five-dimensional expert judgments of song aesthetics.\nBy supervising the reward head with expert ratings, MuseCritic maps information in the critiques to continuous scores that better align with expert assessments. \n\n4.2.2 Out-of-Domain Preference Generalization \n\nTable 2 reports pairwise preference accuracy on Music Arena.\nMuseCritic achieves the highest observed accuracy of 71.35%, which is 0.55, 2.86, and 17.60 percentage points above SongEval, Audiobox Aesthetics, and Qwen3-Omni, respectively.\nTogether with the in-domain results, this observation suggests that the learned rewards retain ranking information under an out-of-domain preference evaluation. \n\n4.3 Downstream Reinforcement Learning \n\nTo test whether MuseCritic provides a useful optimization signal, we use it to train Muse-0.6B ( 12 ) with GRPO ( 19 ) .\nUsing seed 42, we sample 500 multi-turn song-generation examples from the public Muse training set while preserving the original language ratio: 195 Chinese and 305 English examples.\nWe define the reinforcement-learning reward function as the mean of the five aesthetic scores assigned by MuseCritic to each generated song x ~ \\tilde{x} : \n\nr ⁡ ( x ~ ) = 1 K ​ ∑ k = 1 K y ^ k ​ ( x ~ ) . r(\\tilde{x})=\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\hat{y}_{k}(\\tilde{x}). \n\n(11) \n\nWe train for one epoch on six H200 GPUs, sample eight outputs per prompt, and use a learning rate of 10 − 6 10^{-6} .\nAppendix F gives the complete rollout configuration. \n\nEvaluation uses the 100 multi-turn test prompts from Muse ( 12 ) .\nBoth Muse and Muse-GRPO use greedy decoding with T = 0 T=0 .\nAppendix F describes the complete evaluation protocol and robustness checks across evaluation windows.\nAs shown in Table 3 , Muse-GRPO obtains higher observed values than the original model on all nine Audiobox Aesthetics and SongEval metrics.\nThe largest Audiobox gains occur in production complexity (PC, + 0.15 +0.15 ) and content enjoyment (CE, + 0.12 +0.12 ), while all five SongEval dimensions improve by 0.05 0.05 – 0.06 0.06 .\nThe consistent numerical gains across two song-aesthetic evaluators suggest that the MuseCritic reward captures several aesthetic dimensions. \n\nAudiobox Aesthetics ↑ \\uparrow \nSongEval ↑ \\uparrow \n\nModel \nCE \nCU \nPC \nPQ \nCO \nMU \nME \nCL \nNA \n\nMuse \n7.33 \n7.64 \n6.37 \n8.11 \n4.01 \n3.84 \n3.94 \n3.89 \n3.83 \n\nMuse-GRPO \n7.45 \n7.66 \n6.52 \n8.12 \n4.07 \n3.89 \n4.00 \n3.94 \n3.89 \nTable 3: Effect of MuseCritic-guided GRPO on the aesthetic quality of Muse-0.6B. Results use the 100 multi-turn Muse test prompts and T = 0 T=0 decoding. CE/CU/PC/PQ denote Audiobox content enjoyment, content usefulness, production complexity, and production quality. CO/MU/ME/CL/NA denote SongEval coherence, musicality, memorability, structural clarity, and vocal naturalness. Arrows indicate the preferred direction, and bold marks the better observed result in each column. Muse-GRPO yields numerical gains on all nine metrics. Figure 2: Effect of natural-language aesthetic critiques on five-dimensional score distributions. Curves show expert ratings, full MuseCritic predictions, and predictions from the no-critique variant on the fixed SongEval test set. Coherence, Musicality, Memorability, Clarity, and Naturalness correspond to the five aesthetic dimensions. Without critiques, predictions cluster in a narrow high-score region; the full model more closely matches the range of expert ratings. \n\n4.4 Ablation Studies \n\nTable 4 studies three design choices across all five dimensions: natural-language aesthetic critiques, self-generated critiques, and SFT initialization before reward learning.\nAll variants use the same training songs, reward head, and optimization configuration. \n\nDimension \nModel variant \nMSE ↓ \\downarrow \nLCC ↑ \\uparrow \nSRCC ↑ \\uparrow \nKTAU ↑ \\uparrow \n\nCoherence \nMuseCritic \n0.2058 \n0.9147 \n0.8894 \n0.7262 \n\nw/o critique \n0.3881 \n0.9173 \n0.8954 \n0.7326 \n\nOffline critique \n0.4821 \n0.8342 \n0.7566 \n0.5675 \n\nw/o SFT \n0.8556 \n0.6300 \n0.6066 \n0.4689 \n\nMemorability \nMuseCritic \n0.2511 \n0.9098 \n0.8839 \n0.7232 \n\nw/o critique \n0.4936 \n0.9048 \n0.8842 \n0.7219 \n\nOffline critique \n0.6635 \n0.8141 \n0.7590 \n0.5672 \n\nw/o SFT \n1.0037 \n0.6175 \n0.6378 \n0.4823 \n\nVocal naturalness \nMuseCritic \n0.2537 \n0.8906 \n0.8678 \n0.6945 \n\nw/o critique \n0.6186 \n0.8899 \n0.8684 \n0.7034 \n\nOffline critique \n0.5739 \n0.7936 \n0.7527 \n0.5674 \n\nw/o SFT \n0.6469 \n0.6779 \n0.6768 \n0.4978 \n\nStructural clarity \nMuseCritic \n0.2275 \n0.9068 \n0.8806 \n0.7109 \n\nw/o critique \n0.4635 \n0.9041 \n0.8785 \n0.7088 \n\nOffline critique \n0.5033 \n0.8145 \n0.7479 \n0.5526 \n\nw/o SFT \n0.6681 \n0.6869 \n0.6764 \n0.5061 \n\nMusicality \nMuseCritic \n0.2202 \n0.9120 \n0.8972 \n0.7344 \n\nw/o critique \n0.5386 \n0.9080 \n0.8875 \n0.7293 \n\nOffline critique \n0.5175 \n0.8205 \n0.7765 \n0.5877 \n\nw/o SFT \n0.5960 \n0.7354 \n0.7325 \n0.5488 \nTable 4: In-domain ablations of the key MuseCritic training choices. All variants use the same 2,199/200 SongEval split. “w/o critique” predicts scores directly, “Offline critique” trains on Gemini critiques, and “w/o SFT” generates training critiques with the unadapted MOSS-Audio model. The full model combines SFT initialization with self-generated critiques. Arrows indicate the preferred direction, and bold marks the best value for each metric and dimension. \nSelf-generated aesthetic critiques improve absolute-score agreement. \n\nRemoving the critiques increases macro-average MSE from 0.2316 to 0.5005, while the macro-averaged rank correlations remain close.\nFigure 2 explains this difference: the no-critique model concentrates most predictions in a narrow range around 4.\nIt can therefore preserve a coarse ranking among songs without recovering the dynamic range of expert ratings.\nA reward model should preserve not only rank order but also the relative magnitude of quality differences through score intervals.\nAn overly contracted prediction distribution reduces agreement with experts’ absolute ratings and weakens reward discrimination among samples of different quality during downstream policy optimization.\nWith critiques, evaluative polarity and concrete musical descriptions provide an explicit semantic reference, bringing the prediction distribution closer to the expert scale and reducing MSE in every dimension. \n\nTraining on self-generated critiques improves reward prediction. \n\nTo isolate the role of the self-generated dataset 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} , we train a control model on 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} , using external-teacher critiques instead of critiques generated by the target model.\nAs Table 4 shows, the full MuseCritic trained on 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} achieves higher LCC, SRCC, and KTAU than the offline-critique variant on every dimension.\nReplacing 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} with 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} increases macro-average MSE from 0.2316 to 0.5481 and decreases macro-average LCC, SRCC, and KTAU from 0.9068, 0.8838, and 0.7178 to 0.8154, 0.7585, and 0.5685, respectively.\nWe suggest that the external teacher and MOSS-Audio differ in vocabulary, information density, and patterns.\nTraining only on teacher critiques therefore creates a larger mismatch between the critiques observed during reward training and those generated at inference time. \n\nSFT initializes effective critique generation. \n\nWithout SFT, we use the unadapted MOSS-Audio-8B-Instruct model to generate critiques and subsequently train the reward model on these critiques. This variant performs worst overall: macro-averaged MSE increases from 0.2316 to 0.7541, while macro-averaged LCC, SRCC, and KTAU decrease from 0.9068, 0.8838, and 0.7178 to 0.6695, 0.6660, and 0.5008, respectively. The degradation in both absolute-score accuracy and ranking consistency indicates that self-generation alone does not produce a reliable intermediate representation for reward learning. Supervised fine-tuning on teacher critiques is therefore necessary for the critique generator to acquire the evaluation criteria, domain-specific terminology, and rating semantics required to distinguish both the level and relative ordering of song quality. \n\n5 Conclusion \n\nWe introduced MuseCritic, a semi-scalar reward model for the aesthetic evaluation of complete songs.\nThe model treats natural-language aesthetic critiques as intermediate variables: it first organizes evidence along five dimensions and then predicts continuous reward scores.\nTraining initializes critique generation with external-teacher supervision and trains the reward head on self-generated critiques to mitigate the critique distribution shift between training and inference.\nExperiments on SongEval, Music Arena, and three controlled ablations show that SFT-initialized, self-generated critiques reduce absolute scoring error while maintaining strong rank correlations.\nUsing MuseCritic to train Muse with GRPO improves all nine metrics from two song-aesthetic evaluators, connecting critique-conditioned evaluation to preference optimization. \n\nLimitations \n\nThe critique-then-score procedure requires autoregressive critique generation before reward prediction and is therefore more computationally expensive than direct score regression.\nMoreover, long-form song datasets that pair audio with expert aesthetic ratings remain scarce.\nConsequently, MuseCritic is trained primarily on the Chinese and English vocal songs in SongEval.\nFuture work can extend training to a broader range of languages and musical genres. \n\nReferences \n\nAgostinelli et al. (2023) A. Agostinelli, T. I. Denk, Z. Borsos, J. Engel, M. Verzetti, A. Caillon, Q. Huang, A. Jansen, A. Roberts, M. Tagliasacchi, M. Sharifi, N. Zeghidour, and C. Frank MusicLM: generating music from text . arXiv preprint arXiv:2301.11325 . External Links: Link Cited by: §1 , §2.1 . \n\nAnkner et al. (2024) Z. Ankner, M. Paul, B. Cui, J. D. Chang, and P. Ammanabrolu Critique-out-loud reward models . arXiv preprint arXiv:2408.11791 . External Links: Document , Link Cited by: §1 , §2.2 , §3.2 . \n\nBai et al. (2022) Y. Bai, S. Kadavath, S. Kundu, A. Askell, J. Kernion, A. Jones, A. Chen, A. Goldie, A. Mirhoseini, C. McKinnon, et al. Constitutional AI: harmlessness from AI feedback . arXiv preprint arXiv:2212.08073 . External Links: Document , Link Cited by: §3.2 . \n\nCideron et al. (2024) G. Cideron, S. Girgin, M. Verzetti, D. Vincent, M. Kastelic, Z. Borsos, B. McWilliams, V. Ungureanu, O. Bachem, O. Pietquin, M. Geist, L. Hussenot, N. Zeghidour, and A. Agostinelli MusicRL: aligning music generation to human preferences . arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.04229 . External Links: Link Cited by: §2.1 . \n\nCopet et al. (2023) J. Copet, F. Kreuk, I. Gat, T. Remez, D. Kant, G. Synnaeve, Y. Adi, and A. Défossez Simple and controllable music generation . In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems , External Links: Link Cited by: §1 , §2.1 . \n\nDeshmukh et a